DRV.searchIndex = [{"t": "Overview \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html", "x": "Follow one Vulkan call down to the GPU AMD's open-source Vulkan driver, read from its source. Step through what really happens between vkCmdDraw and the command processor, with every excerpt cut from the pinned code and linked to the exact lines. Start with the architecture Look up a function or setting See use cases AMDVLK is discontinued. Its README says so on line 2, and the xgl, pal, llpc and AMD LLVM repositories are archived on GitHub, frozen at their 2025-04 commits. libdrm and the Linux driver are active and newer here (2026), so kernel line numbers match today's kernel. Details are on Sources and status ."}, {"t": "The stack, layer by layer", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#stack", "x": "Select a layer for its responsibilities, its key classes and links into the code. On every topic page a smaller copy follows the stepper and marks the layer the current step runs in."}, {"t": "Reading order", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#start-here", "x": "Each topic is a step-through trace of real code followed by a worked example and the details. Architecture and loader entry How the loader finds the driver, how a VkDevice handle becomes a C++ object, and how XGL objects own PAL objects. The patterns repeat everywhere. Memory allocation The simplest path through all six software layers, and where the BO, VA and residency vocabulary comes from. Command buffers and PM4 What recording a draw writes, down to the bits of a real DRAW_INDEX_AUTO packet. Submission and sync How command chunks become IBs in one CS ioctl, reach a ring, and how fences and semaphores become syncobjs. Shader compilation SPIR-V to LLVM IR to LGC to AMDGPU ISA to an ELF that PAL loads. The deepest topic; read it last."}, {"t": "What the source shows", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#findings", "x": "This PAL and LLPC cannot target GFX9 (Vega). PAL's GfxIpLevel starts at GfxIp10_1 and LLPC asserts gfxIp.major >= 10 , so the site covers GFX10, 11 and 12. PAL's hw/gfxip/gfx9 directory is the GFX10 to 11.5 hardware layer. Device creation sends those generations to Gfx9::CreateDevice . Reserving GPU address space never reaches the kernel. libdrm's amdgpu_va_range_alloc is a user-space allocator; the kernel first sees the address when a BO is mapped there. Every VkDeviceMemory is resident for its whole life. XGL registers each allocation as a device-global reference ( Device::AddMemReference ). Shader modules are not compiled to ISA. Code generation happens per pipeline, because the compiler needs vertex formats, colour formats and the descriptor layout."}, {"t": "Everything else, looked up from the code", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#lookup", "x": "The code reference is extracted from the sources by a script, row by row, with a link to each definition: Vulkan entry points and the call each one forwards to PAL interface methods with their documented purpose driver settings with types, defaults and descriptions libdrm functions PAL binds, libdrm_amdgpu functions and kernel ioctls PM4 opcodes, compiler passes and Vulkan extensions"}, {"t": "How to read a code panel", "p": "Overview", "u": "index.html#how-to-read", "x": "The header shows the repository, file and line range. Open at shows the full file at the pinned commit with those lines highlighted. Excerpts are cut from the source by tools/build.py , never retyped, and are at most 30 lines, so they often stop mid-function. Each panel ends with What this does in plain language. Where something could not be traced, the page says not traced rather than guessing. Dotted underlines are glossary terms. Press / to search pages, steps and the whole code reference. Excerpts are copyright their authors under their original licences: MIT for AMDVLK, xgl, pal and llpc; MIT-style notices on the libdrm and Linux amdgpu files shown; Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions for LLVM. Individual files may carry their own headers."}, {"t": "Architecture &amp; loader entry \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html", "x": "Architecture & loader entry Three AMD libraries make up the driver, and each has one job. XGL speaks Vulkan. PAL speaks GPU and OS. LLPC turns SPIR-V pipelines into machine code. This page shows how they connect and how a Vulkan call enters them."}, {"t": "Who does what", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#roles", "x": "Component Repo / dir Owns Doesn't know about XGL xgl/icd/api Vulkan semantics: entry points, memory types, render passes, descriptor sets, barriers in Vulkan terms, pipeline caches. PM4, registers, ioctls. PAL pal/src/core Hardware and OS: GPU memory, command buffers as PM4, queues, fences. Per-generation HWLs in hw/gfxip/gfx9 and hw/gfxip/gfx12 . OS backend in os/amdgpu . Vulkan. PAL is shared with other AMD drivers. LLPC / LGC llpc/llpc , llpc/lgc SPIR-V \u2192 LLVM IR \u2192 AMDGPU ELF with PAL metadata. Uses AMD's LLVM fork for codegen. GPU memory or submission. It returns bytes; XGL hands them to PAL. The dependency arrows only point one way. XGL calls PAL interfaces ( Pal::IDevice , Pal::IGpuMemory , Pal::ICmdBuffer , Pal::IQueue ) and the LLPC interface ( Llpc::ICompiler ). PAL calls libdrm. The two libraries never call each other: the only thing that moves from LLPC to PAL is the ELF blob, and XGL carries it."}, {"t": "Step-through: from loader to PAL", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Worked example: what happens when an app calls vkCmdDraw", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#example", "x": "PFN_vkCmdDraw pfnDraw = (PFN_vkCmdDraw)vkGetDeviceProcAddr(device, \"vkCmdDraw\"); pfnDraw(cmd, 3, 1, 0, 0); /* or plain vkCmdDraw(cmd, 3, 1, 0, 0) through the loader */ Lookup: vkGetDeviceProcAddr uses the device dispatch table ( vk_dispatch.cpp ). GetEntryPoint finds vkCmdDraw in g_EntryPointMetadataTable , sees it is a DEVICE-level entry point, and returns XGL's vk::entry::vkCmdDraw . Calling the returned pointer skips the loader's trampoline; calling vkCmdDraw directly goes through the loader, which reads the dispatch pointer from the first word of the VkCommandBuffer . Handle to object: VkCommandBuffer is dispatchable, so it points at a Dispatchable<CmdBuffer> . ApiCmdBuffer::ObjectFromHandle skips the VK_LOADER_DATA word to reach the CmdBuffer object ( pointer arithmetic ). XGL to PAL: CmdBuffer::Draw calls ICmdBuffer::CmdDraw on the PAL command buffer it owns, once per GPU in the device mask. PAL to hardware layer: the call jumps through m_funcTable into the GFX10/11 or GFX12 hardware layer chosen at device creation. Topic 4 follows it down to PM4. Every arrow in this chain is a direct call or a table jump in user space. Nothing reaches the kernel until vkQueueSubmit ."}, {"t": "Loader entry and dispatch tables", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#loader", "x": "The Khronos loader reads amd-icd.json , loads the library, and calls vk_icdNegotiateLoaderICDInterfaceVersion . XGL accepts interface versions 3 through 6. From then on, every function pointer the loader hands the application comes from vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr . XGL keeps three kinds of DispatchTable . The global table, g_GlobalDispatchTable , answers before an instance exists. Each instance has a table filtered by the enabled API version and instance extensions, and each device has one filtered by device extensions. Every table is filled by the same DispatchTable::Init , which pairs each name with the internal vk::entry:: implementation."}, {"t": "Handles are pointers; the loader owns the first word", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#handles", "x": "For dispatchable handles ( VkInstance , VkPhysicalDevice , VkDevice , VkQueue , VkCommandBuffer ), the loader writes its own dispatch pointer into the first pointer-sized word of the object. XGL's Dispatchable<C> template reserves that word as VK_LOADER_DATA (seeded with ICD_LOADER_MAGIC ) and stores the real C++ object right after it. ObjectFromHandle converts a handle into a pointer to that object. Non-dispatchable handles ( VkDeviceMemory , VkBuffer , VkPipeline \u2026) need no loader word. NonDispatchable<> just casts the 64-bit handle to a pointer."}, {"t": "XGL objects wrap PAL objects", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#wrapping", "x": "Almost every XGL object holds one PAL object per GPU in the device group, and PAL never allocates CPU memory for API objects itself. XGL first asks PAL how big its object is (e.g. GetGpuMemorySize , GetFenceSize , GetCmdAllocatorSize ), then usually makes one allocation (semaphores are an exception and allocate the two separately) through the application's VkAllocationCallbacks holding [XGL object][PAL object \u00d7 N GPUs] , and finally calls PAL's Create* with a placement address inside it. Vulkan handle XGL class PAL interface it owns VkInstance Instance Pal::IPlatform ( Pal::CreatePlatform ) VkPhysicalDevice / VkDevice PhysicalDevice / Device Pal::IDevice ( Device::PalDevice ) VkDeviceMemory Memory Pal::IGpuMemory VkCommandPool / VkCommandBuffer CmdPool / CmdBuffer Pal::ICmdAllocator / Pal::ICmdBuffer VkQueue Queue Pal::IQueue VkPipeline GraphicsPipeline / ComputePipeline Pal::IPipeline VkFence / VkSemaphore Fence / Semaphore Pal::IFence / Pal::IQueueSemaphore"}, {"t": "PAL picks a hardware layer and an OS layer", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#hwl", "x": "Inside PAL, the generic Pal::Device creates a GfxDevice for the detected GFX IP ( device.cpp ). On Linux, the OS-specific object is an Amdgpu::Device that talks to libdrm through a table of dlsym 'd function pointers:"}, {"t": "Where LLPC is invoked", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#llpc", "x": "Each PhysicalDevice has a PipelineCompiler . During initialization it converts PAL's GfxIpLevel into a Vkgc::GfxIpVersion and creates the Llpc::ICompiler ( compiler_solution_llpc.cpp ). After that, LLPC is called from exactly two places: BuildShaderModule (shader module creation) and BuildGraphicsPipeline / BuildComputePipeline (pipeline creation, on a cache miss). See Topic 3 ."}, {"t": "Which GPU generations?", "p": "Architecture &amp; loader entry", "u": "architecture.html#gfx", "x": "The pinned PAL enumerates only GFX10.1 and newer for current clients: GFX9 (Vega) support was removed from these final AMDVLK sources. The AMDVLK README says \u201cFor Pre-GFX10 GPUs, please use v-2023.Q3.3 or older release.\u201d Everything on this site therefore describes GFX10, GFX11 and GFX12."}, {"t": "Memory allocation \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html", "x": "Memory allocation vkAllocateMemory runs through every software layer: XGL picks a PAL heap, PAL reserves a GPU virtual address, creates a buffer object in the kernel and maps it at that address, and XGL marks the memory resident for every future submission."}, {"t": "Virtual address vs. physical backing: three separate operations", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#three-ops", "x": "On amdgpu, a usable allocation is the result of three independent steps, and PAL performs them separately in Amdgpu::GpuMemory::AllocateOrPinMemory : # What PAL call libdrm Kernel A Reserve a GPU VA range AssignVirtualAddress amdgpu_va_range_alloc none. The VA allocator lives in user space ( dev->va_mgr ). B Create physical backing (a BO in VRAM or GTT) AllocBuffer amdgpu_bo_alloc DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE \u2192 amdgpu_gem_create_ioctl C Map B at A in the process's GPU page tables MapVirtualAddress amdgpu_bo_va_op_raw DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_VA \u2192 amdgpu_gem_va_ioctl \u2192 amdgpu_vm_bo_map Keeping them separate is what makes virtual (sparse/PRT) allocations cheap. For memory created with flags.virtualAlloc , PAL does step A and then only reserves the range as PRT ( Device::ReservePrtVaRange ), with no backing. Sparse binding later maps real BOs into pieces of that range. A VkBuffer or VkImage bound to ordinary memory gets no VA of its own. Its address is the memory's VA plus the bind offset ( Buffer bind )."}, {"t": "Step-through: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 kernel", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Worked examples: three allocations", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#example", "x": "1. A 64 MiB upload buffer the CPU writes every frame VkMemoryAllocateInfo info = { .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_MEMORY_ALLOCATE_INFO, .allocationSize = 64u << 20, .memoryTypeIndex = typeWith(VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT | VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_COHERENT_BIT) }; vkAllocateMemory(device, &info, NULL, &mem); vkMapMemory(device, mem, 0, VK_WHOLE_SIZE, 0, &ptr); ( typeWith stands for the usual \"first memory type with these flags\" loop.) Which type? On a discrete GPU without Resizable BAR, XGL's priority order puts the GartUswc type second, after the device-local Invisible type, so the first HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_COHERENT match is GpuHeapGartUswc . VA: PAL reserves 64 MiB of GPU virtual address space in the default partition ( ChooseVaPartition ); libdrm hands it out in user space. Backing: AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE with domain GTT and AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC . For memory private to the process, and when the kernel supports it, PAL adds AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_VM_ALWAYS_VALID . Map: AMDGPU_GEM_VA maps the BO at the VA. That is the second and last ioctl for the allocation. Residency: XGL calls AddMemReference , but because the BO is always-valid, the per-queue reference maps skip it and it never appears in a BO list. CPU map: vkMapMemory passes PAL's CPU-visible check, then libdrm does one AMDGPU_GEM_MMAP ioctl and an mmap . CPU writes through the pointer are write-combined (fast); reads through it are very slow. 2. A 256 MiB device-local image heap The first DEVICE_LOCAL type is the Invisible type, so heaps[0] = GpuHeapInvisible . AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE with domain VRAM and AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_NO_CPU_ACCESS : the kernel may place it outside the CPU-visible BAR window. Calling vkMapMemory on it (which the Vulkan spec forbids anyway) reaches PAL's ErrorNotMappable branch and returns VK_ERROR_MEMORY_MAP_FAILED ( vk_memory.cpp ). 3. The same requests on a Resizable BAR system PAL reports an Invisible heap of size 0. XGL still exposes the device-local type, but routes it to GpuHeapLocal . PAL's amdgpu layer gives Invisible the same domain and flags as Local, so all VRAM is CPU-mappable."}, {"t": "How heaps and memory types are derived", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#heaps", "x": "PAL describes memory as four heaps . The kernel has two domains . Vulkan exposes memory heaps and memory types . The table connects them, using PAL's default heap flags ( Device::InitMemoryHeapProperties ), XGL's conversion ( PhysicalDevice memory-type setup ) and PAL's amdgpu translation ( heapToDomain / heapToFlags ): PAL heap PAL heap flags (default) Vulkan memory type flags Vulkan heap Kernel domain + create flags GpuHeapInvisible cpuUncached DEVICE_LOCAL VRAM (DEVICE_LOCAL) AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM + AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_NO_CPU_ACCESS GpuHeapLocal cpuVisible, cpuGpuCoherent, cpuUncached, cpuWriteCombined DEVICE_LOCAL | HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_COHERENT CPU-visible VRAM (DEVICE_LOCAL) AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM + AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_ACCESS_REQUIRED | AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC GpuHeapGartUswc cpuVisible, cpuGpuCoherent, cpuUncached, cpuWriteCombined HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_COHERENT system memory AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT + AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC GpuHeapGartCacheable cpuVisible, cpuGpuCoherent HOST_VISIBLE | HOST_COHERENT | HOST_CACHED shares the GartUswc heap AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT , no extra flag (CPU-cached) Order matters. XGL creates heaps and types in the fixed priority order Invisible, GartUswc, Local, GartCacheable, so memory type indices follow that order on a typical discrete GPU. Heap flags. PalGpuHeapToVkMemoryHeapFlags marks Local and Invisible as VK_MEMORY_HEAP_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT . Resizable BAR. When PAL reports an Invisible heap of size 0 (all VRAM CPU-visible), XGL routes the Invisible memory type to GpuHeapLocal , and PAL's amdgpu layer treats Invisible like Local. \u201cLocal\u201d vs. \u201cInvisible\u201d is only a hint to the kernel. Both are VRAM. The CPU_ACCESS_REQUIRED / NO_CPU_ACCESS flags steer placement inside or outside the CPU-visible BAR window, and the PAL comment notes the kernel may change this at runtime. Device-coherent types for VK_AMD_device_coherent_memory are added as extra types when the GPU supports GL2-uncached access ( supportGl2Uncached )."}, {"t": "Residency, memory references and the BO list", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#residency", "x": "On amdgpu, a BO that the GPU will touch must either appear in the submission's BO list or be VM-always-valid . The kernel uses the list to validate (and, if needed, move back) every BO before running the job. Vulkan has no residency API, so XGL takes the simplest correct route: Device::AddMemReference is called for every allocation. It calls IDevice::AddGpuMemoryReferences with pQueue = nullptr , meaning \"device-global\". Amdgpu::Device::AddGpuMemoryReferences adds the memory to every queue's m_globalRefMap . Memory created VM_ALWAYS_VALID is skipped , because the kernel keeps it valid in the process VM. At submit time Queue::UpdateResourceList turns the map into the BO list passed with the CS ioctl (see Topic 5 ). PAL requests AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_VM_ALWAYS_VALID when the kernel supports it and the memory is private to the process (not shareable, not exported, not flippable, not peer-writable). Those BOs share the VM root's reservation object ( kernel side ), so they never need to appear in the BO list."}, {"t": "Mapping host-visible memory", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#mapping", "x": "vkMapMemory \u2192 Memory::Map \u2192 IGpuMemory::Map . PAL refuses virtual memory and anything not CPU-visible ( ErrorNotMappable ), which is why Invisible-heap memory types lack HOST_VISIBLE. For a mappable BO, libdrm's amdgpu_bo_cpu_map asks the kernel ( DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_MMAP ) for the BO's fake mmap offset and then calls mmap on the DRM file descriptor. CPU accesses to VRAM pages go through the PCIe BAR; GTT pages are ordinary system pages, either write-combined (USWC) or cached. How the kernel services the resulting page faults (TTM) is not traced . Mappings are reference-counted inside libdrm per BO. XGL can keep a mapping alive for the object's lifetime with the skipUnMapMemory setting."}, {"t": "Not traced on this page", "p": "Memory allocation", "u": "memory.html#not-traced", "x": "TTM placement, eviction and the VRAM \u2192 GTT fallback inside amdgpu_bo_create . Page-table writing in amdgpu_gem_va_update_vm / amdgpu_vm_bo_update . Imported/exported memory ( OpenExternalMemory , dma-buf) and pinned host memory ( CreateGpuPinnedMemory \u2192 PinMemory ) are only mentioned, not stepped through."}, {"t": "Shader compilation \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html", "x": "Shader compilation AMDVLK compiles pipelines , not shaders. A shader module is only hashed and preprocessed; when a pipeline is created, LLPC translates each stage's SPIR-V to LLVM IR, links the stages into one module, lowers it with full knowledge of the pipeline state, and has LLVM emit a single ELF that PAL loads."}, {"t": "The pipeline in one picture", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#overview", "x": "XGL : vkCreateShaderModule \u2192 PipelineCompiler::BuildShaderModule \u2192 LLPC BuildShaderModule , which hashes the SPIR-V and records usage. No ISA is produced yet. XGL : vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 cache lookups \u2192 ConvertGraphicsPipelineInfo builds a Vkgc::GraphicsPipelineBuildInfo from all the Vulkan state. LLPC front end : per stage, LowerTranslator \u2192 SPIRV reader ( SPIRVToLLVM ) \u2192 front-end lowering passes. The stages are then linked into one pipeline module. LGC middle end : PipelineState::generate \u2192 LgcLowering::addPasses : state-dependent lowering, hardware stage mapping, NGG. LLVM AMDGPU backend : addPassesToEmitFile on target amdgcn--amdpal with CPU gfx10xx / gfx11xx / gfx12xx . It produces ISA plus a PAL metadata note, packaged as an ELF. PAL : IDevice::CreateGraphicsPipeline reads the ELF with PipelineAbiReader , uploads code with CodeObjectUploader , and turns the metadata into register state."}, {"t": "Step-through: shader module \u2192 pipeline \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Worked example: the same pipeline, created twice", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#example", "x": "VkGraphicsPipelineCreateInfo info = { .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_GRAPHICS_PIPELINE_CREATE_INFO, .stageCount = 2, .pStages = stages, /* vertex + fragment SPIR-V */ .pVertexInputState = &vertexInput, /* binding 0: R32G32B32_SFLOAT position */ .pColorBlendState = &blend, .layout = layout, /* render target: B8G8R8A8_UNORM */ /* ... */ }; vkCreateGraphicsPipelines(device, cache, 1, &info, NULL, &pipeA); vkCreateGraphicsPipelines(device, cache, 1, &info, NULL, &pipeB); /* identical */ First creation: everything misses Cache lookups: the shader modules were hashed at vkCreateShaderModule . GetCachedPipelineBinary misses in cache and in XGL's internal binary cache. State capture: ConvertGraphicsPipelineInfo records, among other things, the vertex format ( BuildVertexInputInterfaceState ), the colour format and blend ( BuildFragmentOutputInterfaceState ) and the descriptor layout ( BuildPipelineResourceMapping ). LLPC: each stage's SPIR-V becomes LLVM IR, the two modules are linked, and LGC lowers them. The state above is consumed here: LowerVertexFetch generates loads that unpack three floats, LowerFragmentColorExport converts the output to the B8G8R8A8 export format, and LowerDesc turns resource accesses into loads relative to user-data pointers. Codegen: on a GFX11 GPU the target is gfx1100 (for example), NGG is forced on, and the vertex shader becomes part of an NGG primitive shader. LLVM emits ISA plus PAL metadata into one ELF. PAL: the ELF is uploaded to GPU memory and the register values are prepared. On a cache-enabled device the ELF is also stored in the caches. Second creation: one lookup The cache ID is the same, so GetCachedPipelineBinary hits (in cache if the app passed one, otherwise in XGL's internal cache) and LLPC is never called. Only the PAL pipeline object is created again from the cached ELF. What a different state would do Change the render-target format to R16G16B16A16_SFLOAT and the colour export code changes, so it is a new pipeline compile. LGC's per-stage cache ( CheckShaderCache ) is designed to let the unchanged vertex stage be reused."}, {"t": "SPIR-V \u2192 LLVM IR", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#spirv", "x": "LowerTranslator is an LLVM module pass that runs the SPIRV reader on one shader stage. Before translating, it optionally runs a SPIR-V optimizer and builds the specialization-constant map from VkSpecializationInfo . llvm::readSpirv parses the binary into a SPIRVModule , and SPIRVToLLVM::translate picks the requested entry point and emits IR. The translator is constructed with an lgc::Builder , so resource and built-in operations become LGC operations that are resolved later, not raw hardware intrinsics. Front-end lowering ( Lowering::addPasses ) then cleans up SPIR-V constructs: CFG merges, aggressive inlining, access chains, terminators, globals, memory operations, math precision. Once every stage is lowered, the per-stage modules are linked into one pipeline module."}, {"t": "LGC: pipeline-state-dependent lowering", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#lgc", "x": "LGC receives the pipeline state that XGL converted (vertex input formats, color target formats and blend, descriptor layout as user-data nodes, GFX IP, options) through setPipelineState . Its pass list ( LgcLowering::addPasses ) is where that state is consumed: Pass Needs which pipeline state LowerVertexFetch Vertex binding/attribute formats and strides, to generate fetch code for VS inputs. LowerFragmentColorExport Color attachment formats and blend, to pick export formats and conversions. LowerDesc Descriptor layout (resource mapping), to turn descriptor accesses into loads from user data/descriptor tables. LowerInOut Which stages exist and how they are merged, to lower inter-stage I/O. PreparePipelineAbi Which stages exist, to merge API stages into hardware stages and lay out the PAL ABI. CheckShaderCache Runs after CollectResourceUsage , so a stage can be skipped if an identical one was already compiled."}, {"t": "Where caching happens", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#caching", "x": "Pipeline creation tries four lookups, roughly from cheapest to most expensive to miss: # Cache Key Code 1 XGL shader-module cache (in-memory map, then the internal binary cache) hash of SPIR-V LoadShaderModuleFromCache 2 XGL pipeline-binary cache: the app's VkPipelineCache first, then XGL's internal PipelineBinaryCache pipeline cache ID (MetroHash of create info) GetCachedPipelineBinary 3 LLPC whole-pipeline cache ( CacheFullPipelines , on by default) LLPC cacheHash BuildGraphicsPipeline 4 LGC per-stage cache ( EnablePerStageCache , on by default) per-stage hash CheckShaderCache pass LLPC's \"internal\" caches are XGL's PipelineBinaryCache exposed through a Vkgc::ICache adapter ( GetCacheAdapter ), unless the shaderCacheMode setting disables it. The AMDVLK README documents on-disk caching under ~/.cache/AMD/VkCache (setting AllowVkPipelineCachingToDisk ). PAL's archive-file layer behind that is not traced here."}, {"t": "AMDGPU backend \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#backend", "x": "LGC creates an LLVM TargetMachine for triple amdgcn--amdpal with a CPU name derived from the GFX IP ( getGpuNameString : {11,0,0} \u2192 gfx1100 ) and calls addPassesToEmitFile . Inside LLVM, AMDGPUAsmPrinter::EmitPALMetadata records each hardware stage's entry symbol and register usage into AMDGPUPALMetadata , which is written as a MsgPack note ( toBlob ). The instruction selection, register allocation and scheduling that produce the ISA are standard LLVM codegen and are not traced on this site. PAL never interprets the ISA. PipelineAbiReader finds the symbols and metadata; CodeObjectUploader copies the code into GPU memory and applies relocations; the HWL ( Gfx9 / Gfx12 GraphicsPipeline::HwlInit ) builds the register values it will later write with SET_SH_REG / SET_CONTEXT_REG when the pipeline is bound."}, {"t": "How the GFX IP level changes the output", "p": "Shader compilation", "u": "shaders.html#gfx", "x": "Aspect GFX10 (10.1, 10.3) GFX11 (11.0, 11.5) GFX12 Where LLVM CPU gfx1010 \u2026 gfx1036 gfx1100 \u2026 gfx1151 gfx1201 GpuNameMap NGG (primitive shaders) on unless NggFlagDisable mandatory mandatory LgcLowering Extra LGC passes \u2014 CollectImageOperations CollectImageOperations , AddBufferOperationMetadata \u2265 11 , \u2265 12 Buffer format encodings e.g. BUF_FORMAT_32_32_32_32_FLOAT_GFX10 e.g. BUF_FORMAT_32_32_32_32_FLOAT_GFX11 PreparePipelineAbi PAL HWL that loads it Gfx9::GraphicsPipeline (dir hw/gfxip/gfx9 ) Gfx12::GraphicsPipeline HWL selection GFX9 (Vega) Not supported by these sources: LLPC asserts gfxIp.major >= 10 ; no GfxIp9 for current PAL clients. ICompiler::Create Beyond these switches, most generation differences sit inside the LLVM backend (instruction encodings and scheduling models such as GFX12SpeedModel ) and are not traced here."}, {"t": "Command buffers &amp; PM4 \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html", "x": "Command buffers & PM4 Recording a Vulkan command buffer means PAL writing PM4 packets into chunks of GPU-visible memory. Nothing reaches the kernel until submission. This page follows vkCmdDraw down to the three DWORDs it produces and takes them apart bit by bit."}, {"t": "Pools, allocators and chunks", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#chunks", "x": "Each VkCommandPool owns a PAL ICmdAllocator ( vk_cmd_pool.cpp ). The allocator manages separate pools for several kinds of data. All are GPU memory carved into uniform chunks : CommandDataAlloc holds the PM4 command streams themselves. EmbeddedDataAlloc / LargeEmbeddedDataAlloc hold \"embedded data\": tables and constants that PAL writes at record time for commands to reference (the large variant is for allocations over 32 KB). GpuScratchMemAlloc is GPU-only scratch for PAL's internal use. A CmdStream asks for space with ReserveCommands and gives back what it didn't use with CommitCommands . When a chunk fills, CmdStream::GetNextChunk takes one of the stream's retained chunks or calls CmdAllocator::GetNewChunk . The allocator pops its free list (known idle), then scans its reuse list for chunks whose GPU work has finished ( IsIdleOnGpu ), and only then creates a new CmdStreamAllocation , split into numChunks chunks. Successive chunks of one stream are chained with chaining packets at each chunk's tail, so at submit time only the stream's first chunk is handed to the kernel as an IB (see Topic 5 )."}, {"t": "The draw hot path", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#hot-path", "x": "XGL does little at draw time. CmdBuffer::Draw flushes any changed Vulkan dynamic state into PAL ( ValidateGraphicsStates ) and calls ICmdBuffer::CmdDraw . That call is an inline jump through m_funcTable.pfnCmdDraw , which the HWL fills with a template instantiation specialized for the GPU and the command buffer's options. The HWL's CmdDraw : Validates ( ValidateDraw ): writes dirty state as SET_SH_REG / SET_CONTEXT_REG packets. This covers the bound pipeline's registers, user-data SGPRs (descriptor table addresses, push constants), viewport/scissor, and draw-time values such as NUM_INSTANCES and the vertex offset . The full register sequence is not traced line by line. Builds the draw packet into reserved space: DRAW_INDEX_AUTO for non-indexed draws, IT_DRAW_INDEX_2 for indexed, IT_DRAW_INDIRECT / IT_DRAW_INDEX_INDIRECT or IT_EXECUTE_INDIRECT__EXECINDIRECT variants for indirect draws. Commits the space."}, {"t": "Step-through: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Worked example: the PM4 for one draw and one dispatch", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#example", "x": "vkBeginCommandBuffer(cmd, &begin); vkCmdBindPipeline(cmd, VK_PIPELINE_BIND_POINT_GRAPHICS, gfxPipeline); vkCmdDraw(cmd, 3, 1, 0, 0); vkCmdBindPipeline(cmd, VK_PIPELINE_BIND_POINT_COMPUTE, csPipeline); vkCmdDispatch(cmd, 64, 1, 1); vkEndCommandBuffer(cmd); The draw, on a GFX10/11 GPU Validation packets first. ValidateDraw writes whatever is dirty: the pipeline's shader and context registers ( SET_SH_REG and SET_CONTEXT_REG , or packed register pairs on GFX11), user-data SGPRs (descriptor table pointers, the vertex offset), viewport and scissor. Because the instance count changed from \"unknown\" to 1, it also emits NUM_INSTANCES : header 0xC0002F00 (opcode 0x2F, count 0), then 0x00000001 . The draw packet: 0xC0012D00 0x00000003 0x00000002 . That is the type-3 header for IT_DRAW_INDEX_AUTO , a vertex count of 3, and VGT_DRAW_INITIATOR with SOURCE_SELECT = auto-index. Reproduce it in the packet widget . The dispatch Validation: ValidateDispatchPalAbi writes the compute shader's registers and user data. The packet: 5 DWORDs ( PM4_ME_DISPATCH_DIRECT_SIZEDW__CORE = 5). Header 0xC0031502 : type 3, count 5 \u2212 2 = 3, opcode IT_DISPATCH_DIRECT = 0x15, shader type 1 ( ShaderCompute ). dim_x = 64, dim_y = 1, dim_z = 1. dispatch_initiator = 0x00000045 for a wave64 shader: COMPUTE_SHADER_EN (bit 0), FORCE_START_AT_000 (bit 2, because the universal command buffer instantiates BuildDispatchDirect<false, true> ) and ORDER_MODE (bit 6). A wave32 shader adds CS_W32_EN (bit 15) to give 0x00008045 ( wave32 bit ). Where it all lives All of these DWORDs go into the command buffer's draw-engine stream ( m_deCmdStream ), in a chunk from the pool's CommandDataAlloc . At vkEndCommandBuffer the stream is closed, and at submit its first chunk becomes one IB ( Topic 5 )."}, {"t": "Anatomy of a real packet: DRAW_INDEX_AUTO", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#pm4", "x": "This is the packet CmdUtil::BuildDrawIndexAuto writes for vkCmdDraw . The field layouts come from PAL's generated chip headers ( PM4_ME_TYPE_3_HEADER , PM4_PFP_DRAW_INDEX_AUTO , VGT_DRAW_INITIATOR ). Change the inputs to see the DWORDs change, and click a field to see what it means. Where the other fields come from: the instance count and first vertex/instance are not in this packet. PAL wrote them during validation as an IT_NUM_INSTANCES packet and as user-data SGPRs that the compiled vertex shader reads. That is why DRAW_INDEX_AUTO needs only a vertex count."}, {"t": "Compute: DISPATCH_DIRECT", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#dispatch", "x": "vkCmdDispatch follows the same shape: CmdBuffer::Dispatch \u2192 ICmdBuffer::CmdDispatch \u2192 HWL CmdDispatch \u2192 CmdUtil::BuildDispatchDirect . That builds a 5-DWORD packet (header, dim_x , dim_y , dim_z , dispatch_initiator ) with shaderType = ShaderCompute . On a compute-only queue the same builder writes into a ComputeCmdBuffer stream that runs on the MEC."}, {"t": "Barriers: ACQUIRE_MEM and RELEASE_MEM", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#barriers", "x": "On GFX10+ XGL expresses vkCmdPipelineBarrier in PAL's acquire/release model ( useReleaseAcquire ). PAL's BarrierMgr turns the source stages into release events (e.g. a bottom-of-pipe RELEASE_MEM event, optionally with cache write-back) and the destination stages into an acquire point (how early in the pipe the CP must wait). Cache invalidations go into an ACQUIRE_MEM . Image layout transitions may add decompression or clear blits through PAL's internal RPM ( hw/gfxip/rpm ), which is not traced here. One generation difference is visible in the code: on GFX11 the color/depth (RB) caches can no longer be flushed by an ACQUIRE_MEM ( assert )."}, {"t": "GFX12 differences", "p": "Command buffers &amp; PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#gfx12", "x": "GFX12 has its own HWL ( hw/gfxip/gfx12 ) with its own UniversalCmdBuffer , CmdUtil and packet headers. The draw packet is built the same way, but register writes are organized around pair packets:"}, {"t": "Submission &amp; sync \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html", "x": "Submission & synchronization Submission turns recorded command chunks into one CS ioctl. The ioctl carries IB descriptors, a BO list and syncobj wait/signal lists. The kernel validates it, queues a job, and eventually writes an INDIRECT_BUFFER packet into a hardware ring. Fences and semaphores are DRM syncobjs throughout."}, {"t": "The path in one paragraph", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#overview", "x": "vkQueueSubmit \u2192 Queue::Submit (XGL) queues the semaphore waits, collects each command buffer's Pal::ICmdBuffer , and calls IQueue::Submit . PAL's core Queue::SubmitInternal associates fences and calls OsSubmit . The amdgpu backend builds the BO list from residency references, turns each command stream into an IB, packs IB + syncobj + BO-list chunks, and calls libdrm amdgpu_cs_submit_raw2 , which issues DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS . In the kernel, amdgpu_cs_ioctl parses and validates and pushes a job to the DRM GPU scheduler. When its dependencies resolve, amdgpu_job_run \u2192 amdgpu_ib_schedule writes the IB pointers and a fence into the ring."}, {"t": "Step-through: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 ring, then fences & semaphores", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Worked example: one submit with a timeline wait, a semaphore signal and a fence", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#example", "x": "VkTimelineSemaphoreSubmitInfo tl = { .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_TIMELINE_SEMAPHORE_SUBMIT_INFO, .waitSemaphoreValueCount = 1, .pWaitSemaphoreValues = &(uint64_t){ 5 } }; VkPipelineStageFlags stage = VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_ALL_COMMANDS_BIT; VkSubmitInfo submit = { .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_SUBMIT_INFO, .pNext = &tl, .waitSemaphoreCount = 1, .pWaitSemaphores = &uploadTimeline, .pWaitDstStageMask = &stage, .commandBufferCount = 1, .pCommandBuffers = &cmd, .signalSemaphoreCount = 1, .pSignalSemaphores = &renderDone }; vkQueueSubmit(queue, 1, &submit, frameFence); Wait, recorded not performed: PalWaitSemaphores calls WaitQueueSemaphore(uploadTimeline, 5) . PAL's amdgpu queue just pushes {syncobj, 5} onto m_waitSemList . Submit: XGL builds a Pal::SubmitInfo with one command buffer and the fence. OsSubmit refreshes the BO list if needed, and SubmitPm4 adds IBs: PAL's internal preamble stream(s), then the command buffer's first chunk, then any postamble. One ioctl: SubmitIbsRaw builds the chunk array: AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_IB per IB, AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT {uploadTimeline, 5}, a signal chunk containing the queue's m_lastSignaledSyncObject , and the BO list as AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES . Then one DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS . Kernel: the timeline wait becomes a scheduler dependency. The job runs once point 5 signals, its IBs are emitted as INDIRECT_BUFFER packets on the GFX ring, and the job's fence signals the queue's syncobj when the GPU finishes. After the ioctl: PAL copies the fence of m_lastSignaledSyncObject into frameFence 's syncobj ( DoAssociateFenceWithLastSubmit ), and XGL's PalSignalSemaphores does the same for renderDone ( Queue::SignalSemaphore \u2192 ConveySyncObjectState ). Waiting on the CPU: vkWaitForFences(frameFence) becomes amdgpu_cs_syncobj_wait on that syncobj. Nothing polls the GPU. In total: one CS ioctl and two syncobj-transfer ioctls, and no CPU blocking anywhere in vkQueueSubmit ."}, {"t": "IBs and rings", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#ibs", "x": "An IB is just {va_start, ib_bytes, ip_type, ring, flags} ( drm_amdgpu_cs_chunk_ib ): a pointer to PM4 that PAL already wrote into GPU memory. PAL submits one IB per command stream: the stream's first chunk, with the remaining chunks reached by chaining. The IP type selects the ring family: Vulkan queue family PAL queue/engine ip_type Hardware GRAPHICS | COMPUTE | TRANSFER | SPARSE QueueTypeUniversal / EngineTypeUniversal AMDGPU_HW_IP_GFX GFX ring (CP PFP/ME) COMPUTE | TRANSFER | SPARSE QueueTypeCompute / EngineTypeCompute AMDGPU_HW_IP_COMPUTE compute rings (MEC / ACE) TRANSFER | SPARSE QueueTypeDma / EngineTypeDma AMDGPU_HW_IP_DMA SDMA ring (SDMA packets, not PM4) Each PAL queue owns a kernel context ( amdgpu_cs_ctx_create2 , via CreateCommandSubmissionContextRaw ). The CS ioctl names that context, and the kernel returns a per-context sequence number that identifies the submission."}, {"t": "BO lists", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#bo-list", "x": "Every BO the GPU may access must be in the submission's BO list, unless it was created VM_ALWAYS_VALID . PAL builds the list in UpdateResourceList from the device-global references XGL registered at allocation time ( Topic 2 ) and reuses the previous list when nothing changed. On current kernels the list goes inline in the CS ioctl as an AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES chunk. On the legacy path PAL first creates a kernel list object ( DRM_AMDGPU_BO_LIST , via amdgpu_bo_list_create_raw ) and passes its handle instead. In the kernel, amdgpu_cs_parser_bos locks and validates every listed BO before the job may run."}, {"t": "Fences, semaphores, timelines \u2192 syncobjs", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#sync", "x": "Vulkan XGL \u2192 PAL amdgpu backend GPU-side wait / signal Host-side wait / signal VkFence Fence \u2192 Pal::IFence SyncobjFence (one syncobj); legacy TimestampFence on old kernels signal: after the CS, the queue's m_lastSignaledSyncObject fence is transferred into the fence's syncobj vkWaitForFences \u2192 WaitForSyncobjFences \u2192 amdgpu_cs_syncobj_wait binary VkSemaphore Semaphore \u2192 Pal::IQueueSemaphore QueueSemaphore (one syncobj) wait: AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT chunk at point 0; signal: transfer from the last-submit syncobj \u2014 (binary semaphores have no host operations) timeline VkSemaphore same, with flags.timeline same syncobj used as a timeline wait: AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT at the requested point; signal: transfer into the requested point vkWaitSemaphores / vkSignalSemaphore \u2192 WaitSemaphoreValue / SignalSemaphoreValue \u2192 amdgpu_cs_syncobj_timeline_wait / amdgpu_cs_syncobj_timeline_signal The design rests on one trick: every CS ioctl signals the queue's private syncobj ( m_lastSignaledSyncObject , appended to the signal chunk). Anything that must signal when that submission finishes, whether a VkFence or a Vulkan signal semaphore, gets a copy of that fence through ConveySyncObjectState . That function uses amdgpu_cs_syncobj_transfer , or a sync-file export/import as a fallback. Waits are never done on the CPU: they become kernel scheduler dependencies, so a submission blocked on a semaphore costs no CPU time. Ioctl-level detail for the syncobj chunks is in the kernel's amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_in , amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_timeline_wait , amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_out and amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_timeline_signal ( amdgpu_cs.c )."}, {"t": "The kernel side", "p": "Submission &amp; sync", "u": "submit.html#kernel", "x": "amdgpu_cs_ioctl runs a fixed sequence: amdgpu_cs_parser_init \u2192 amdgpu_cs_pass1 (copy and parse chunks) \u2192 amdgpu_cs_pass2 (IBs, syncobj dependencies) \u2192 amdgpu_cs_parser_bos (lock and validate BOs) \u2192 amdgpu_cs_patch_jobs \u2192 amdgpu_cs_vm_handling (page tables up to date) \u2192 amdgpu_cs_sync_rings \u2192 amdgpu_cs_submit , which calls drm_sched_entity_push_job . The DRM scheduler later calls amdgpu_job_run , which calls amdgpu_ib_schedule . For each IB, the ring's emit_ib (e.g. gfx_v11_0_ring_emit_ib_gfx ) writes an INDIRECT_BUFFER packet with the IB address and the process's VMID. The CP then executes PAL's PM4 using the process's page tables. The kernel checkout is newer (2026) than the AMDVLK sources (2025-04). Newer uAPI (e.g. AMDGPU_USERQ user-mode queues, visible in the ioctl table) is never issued by PAL's amdgpu backend in these sources."}, {"t": "Glossary \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html", "x": "Glossary Elsewhere on the site, these terms are dotted-underlined. Hover over one, or focus it with the keyboard, to see the definition."}, {"t": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html", "x": "Sources, SHAs & status Every code panel and source link on this site points at the commits below. Excerpts are cut from these checkouts by tools/build.py , and tools/verify.py checks every identifier and reference against them."}, {"t": "Pinned commits", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html#shas", "x": "The same table, with branch names and dates, is in src/VERSIONS.md . xgl, pal and llpc were cloned from branch dev , which AMDVLK's default.xml manifest pins; at clone time dev and master pointed to the same final commit. The LLVM fork is the manifest's amd-gfx-gpuopen-dev branch, sparse-checked-out to llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU . Linux is a sparse checkout of drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu and include/uapi/drm ."}, {"t": "Archive / discontinuation status (checked 2026-10-14)", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html#status", "x": "Repository GitHub \u201carchived\u201d README / notes Last commit AMDVLK no README line 2: \u201cThis project is discontinued\u201d (links discussion #416) 2025-09-15 xgl archived no notice in README 2025-04-29 pal archived no notice in README 2025-04-29 llpc archived no notice in README 2025-04-29 llvm-project (GPUOpen) archived AMD's LLVM fork used by LLPC 2025-04-22 libdrm (freedesktop GitLab) active \u2014 2026-09-17 linux (torvalds) active \u2014 2026-10-14 Because libdrm and Linux are much newer than the frozen AMDVLK code, the kernel and libdrm line numbers match today's code, which may differ from the versions AMDVLK last shipped against. The interfaces used here (GEM create/VA/mmap, CS with chunks, syncobjs) are long-standing uAPI."}, {"t": "Not traced, or traced only to the entry point", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html#not-traced", "x": "Vulkan loader internals : the Khronos loader isn't in any cloned repo. Only the ICD interface XGL exports is shown. PAL ValidateDraw register sequence : shown at the call site only. LLVM AMDGPU instruction selection, register allocation and scheduling : entry points only ( addPassesToEmitFile , AMDGPUAsmPrinter , PAL metadata). gpurt (ray-tracing library), MetroHash and CWPack : listed in the manifest but not cloned. Ray-tracing pipelines are out of scope. PAL's on-disk archive-file pipeline cache : mentioned only. Kernel memory management (TTM placement and eviction, page-table updates, mmap fault path) and the GPU scheduler's internals: entry functions only. GFX9 (Vega) : support was removed from these sources, so there is nothing to trace. Barrier-driven decompression blits (RPM) and image layout handling : not stepped through."}, {"t": "How this site was built and checked", "p": "Sources, SHAs &amp; status", "u": "versions.html#method", "x": "Read the code and wrote the call chains ( PLAN.md ). tools/spec.py describes each step by anchor text , not line number. tools/build.py finds the anchor in the source, cuts the excerpt (\u2264 30 lines), checks that the listed symbols appear inside it, and builds a SHA-pinned permalink. tools/verify.py extracts every <code> identifier and every reference from the pages and the generated data, and checks each against the checkouts. It also checks internal links and anchors. A headless Chromium run clicks through the diagram, every stepper, search, the PM4 widget and dark mode."}, {"t": "Descriptor sets \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Descriptor sets", "u": "descriptors.html", "x": "Descriptor sets A descriptor set is a block of GPU memory full of hardware descriptors (SRDs). XGL writes those bytes from the CPU, puts the set's address into a few shader registers when you bind it, and the compiled shader loads descriptors from that address. This page follows one uniform buffer from layout to ISA."}, {"t": "The model in four sentences", "p": "Descriptor sets", "u": "descriptors.html#model", "x": "Layout: each binding gets a fixed size and offset inside the set's memory, from PAL's SRD sizes ( DescriptorSetLayout::Create ). Pool and allocation: a pool is one CPU-mapped GPU allocation, and allocating a set reserves a range of it. Update: writing a descriptor is a CPU store of 16 or 32 bytes into that range. No command, no ioctl. Bind and use: binding records the set's GPU address as user data. At draw time PAL writes it into the shader's user SGPRs, and the shader loads SRDs relative to it."}, {"t": "Step-through: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "p": "Descriptor sets", "u": "descriptors.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Build a buffer SRD", "p": "Descriptor sets", "u": "descriptors.html#srd", "x": "This is the descriptor vkUpdateDescriptorSets writes for a VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_STORAGE_BUFFER on GFX10/11. The fields and constants are the ones PAL's CreateUntypedBufferViewSrds uses. Change the address, range or stride and watch the four DWORDs change."}, {"t": "Worked example: one uniform buffer, end to end", "p": "Descriptor sets", "u": "descriptors.html#example", "x": "An application has a 256-byte uniform block at offset 512 of a buffer, bound as set 0, binding 0, and a fragment shader that reads it. VkDescriptorSetLayoutBinding binding = { .binding = 0, .descriptorType = VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_UNIFORM_BUFFER, .descriptorCount = 1, .stageFlags = VK_SHADER_STAGE_FRAGMENT_BIT }; /* ... vkCreateDescriptorSetLayout, vkCreateDescriptorPool, vkAllocateDescriptorSets ... */ VkDescriptorBufferInfo info = { .buffer = ubo, .offset = 512, .range = 256 }; VkWriteDescriptorSet write = { .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_WRITE_DESCRIPTOR_SET, .dstSet = set, .dstBinding = 0, .descriptorCount = 1, .descriptorType = VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_UNIFORM_BUFFER, .pBufferInfo = &info }; vkUpdateDescriptorSets(device, 1, &write, 0, NULL); vkCmdBindDescriptorSets(cmd, VK_PIPELINE_BIND_POINT_GRAPHICS, layout, 0, 1, &set, 0, NULL); vkCmdDraw(cmd, 3, 1, 0, 0); What the driver does Layout: binding 0 is a uniform buffer, so its static size is untypedBufferView (a 16-byte BufferSrd on GFX10/11). The set's static section is 4 DWORDs. Allocate: the pool's bump allocator returns an aligned offset; the set's CPU and GPU addresses are the pool's base plus that offset. Update: WriteBufferInfoDescriptors computes gpuAddr = buffer VA + 512 and range 256, then PAL encodes the SRD straight into the set's memory. Put the buffer's address plus 512 into the SRD builder above, with range 256: DW2 is 0x00000100 and DW3 is the fixed raw-buffer pattern. Bind: the set's GPU address goes into setBindingData and, through CmdSetUserData , into PAL's user-data entries at the pipeline layout's setBindingRegBase . No PM4 is written yet. Draw: ValidateDraw finds the entries dirty and emits a SET_SH_REG that puts the pointer into the pixel shader's user SGPRs ( WriteUserDataEntriesToSgprs ), then the DRAW_INDEX_AUTO from Topic 4 . Shader: when the pipeline was compiled, LGC's LowerDesc turned the uniform-block access into \"load 4 DWORDs at (descriptor table pointer + binding offset)\", using the resource mapping XGL built from the same pipeline layout ( BuildPipelineResourceMapping ). Variations VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_UNIFORM_BUFFER_DYNAMIC : takes no space in the set. At bind time XGL patches the dynamic offset into the SRD and puts the whole descriptor in user data, so changing the offset per draw needs no memory write. Sampled image: the 32-byte ImageSrd was built at vkCreateImageView ; the update is a memcpy . Null descriptor: a VK_NULL_HANDLE view writes zeros. For buffers PAL also leaves DW3 = 0 when the address is 0 (try address 0 above). Push constants: same user-data mechanism, without any memory at all."}, {"t": "Why pipelines depend on the pipeline layout", "p": "Descriptor sets", "u": "descriptors.html#why-layout", "x": "The compiled ISA hard-codes which SGPRs hold which set pointers and the offset of every binding. Both come from the VkPipelineLayout through the resource mapping. That's why a pipeline must be used with a compatible layout, and why changing the layout means compiling a new pipeline."}, {"t": "Generation differences", "p": "Descriptor sets", "u": "descriptors.html#gfx", "x": "GFX12 has its own SRD encodings ( sq_buf_rsrc_t and sq_img_rsrc_t in the gfx12 chip headers); the builder above shows the GFX10/11 layout. The descriptor sizes an application sees through VK_EXT_descriptor_buffer are exactly PAL's srdSizes , so they can change with the generation."}, {"t": "Presentation \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Presentation", "u": "present.html", "x": "Presentation Presenting is where the driver hands a finished image to someone else: the X server or a Wayland compositor. This page follows vkAcquireNextImageKHR and vkQueuePresentKHR from XGL into PAL's swap chain, its present scheduler, and the DRI3 and Wayland window systems."}, {"t": "The model", "p": "Presentation", "u": "present.html#model", "x": "Images rotate. The swap chain owns N presentable images. Acquire gives you a free one (the least recently used), present gives it back, and an idle fence says when the compositor is done with it. PAL does the bookkeeping, the window system does the display. The PAL swap chain and present scheduler track which images are free and in which order; a window-system object speaks DRI3/Present (X11) or the Wayland protocol. Synchronization is syncobjs again. With explicit sync, each image has acquire and release timeline syncobjs shared with the compositor, the same DRM objects as Topic 5 ."}, {"t": "Step-through: acquire \u2192 present \u2192 X11 / Wayland", "p": "Presentation", "u": "present.html#trace", "x": ""}, {"t": "Worked example: one frame in FIFO mode", "p": "Presentation", "u": "present.html#example", "x": "uint32_t index; vkAcquireNextImageKHR(device, swapchain, UINT64_MAX, imageAvailable, VK_NULL_HANDLE, &index); /* record and submit rendering that waits on imageAvailable and signals renderDone */ VkPresentInfoKHR present = { .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_PRESENT_INFO_KHR, .waitSemaphoreCount = 1, .pWaitSemaphores = &renderDone, .swapchainCount = 1, .pSwapchains = &swapchain, .pImageIndices = &index }; vkQueuePresentKHR(queue, &present); Acquire: PAL's generic path waits on m_availableImageSemaphore and returns the least recently used free index. imageAvailable becomes a PAL queue semaphore, which on amdgpu is a DRM syncobj, so your rendering waits for it on the GPU, not on the CPU. Render: your submission waits on imageAvailable as an AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT chunk and signals renderDone . Present: Queue::Present turns the wait on renderDone into a queue wait, then calls PresentSwapChain . PAL validates that the image is presentable and passes it to its present scheduler. To the window system: on Linux the scheduler calls the window system's Present with the image's idle fence. On X11 that is xcb_present_pixmap (or the synced variant with timeline syncobjs); on Wayland it is attach, damage, commit. FIFO: PAL waits until the request has reached hardware ( WaitForLastImagePresented ), which is how FIFO throttles the application to the display rate. Mode differences, from the code Mailbox: acquire searches for unused images ( ReclaimUnusedImages ) and the present is complete as soon as the image is handed over. Immediate: the only Linux mode that may present inline on the calling thread. FIFO: waits for the last present to reach hardware before completing. When no hardware-accelerated path is available, PAL has a CPU blit fallback ( DoCpuPresentBlit )."}, {"t": "Not traced on this page", "p": "Presentation", "u": "present.html#not-traced", "x": "How the swap-chain images are created and exported as DRI3 pixmaps or wl_buffer s. The compositor's side, and the kernel's KMS page flip once the X server or compositor scans the image out. Fullscreen and direct-display paths ( VK_KHR_display )."}, {"t": "Use cases and settings \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Use cases and settings", "u": "usecases.html", "x": "Use cases and driver settings Concrete things you can do with this driver, each tied to the setting that enables it and the line of code that reads that setting. Every setting name below is checked against the settings files at the pinned commit."}, {"t": "How settings are applied", "p": "Use cases and settings", "u": "usecases.html#how", "x": "The AMDVLK README says to put one name,value pair per line in amdVulkanSettings.cfg or amdPalSettings.cfg , in /etc/amd or the directory named by $AMD_CONFIG_DIR . Lines starting with ; are comments, and settings are read only when a device is created, so restart the application after editing. Enum settings take the number shown in the code reference ; bitmask settings take the sum of their bits. Every XGL and PAL setting, with its type, default, allowed values and description, is in the settings tab . The README is out of date in two places. It lists EnableVmAlwaysValid and AllowVkPipelineCachingToDisk , but neither name exists in any settings file at the pinned commits. Their entries below were replaced by the settings that do exist."}, {"t": "Debugging correctness", "p": "Use cases and settings", "u": "usecases.html#debug", "x": "Find the submission that hangs or corrupts the GPU When a GPU hang or corrupted frame can't be pinned to one command buffer. WaitAfterSubmit,1 XGL then waits for the queue to go idle after every PAL submission. With multiSubmitChaining off, PalQueueSubmit also submits command buffers one at a time, so a hang shows up right after the offending one. Expect a large slowdown. Setting: WaitAfterSubmit , MultiSubmitChaining . Read at: , The README also suggests IdleAfterSubmitGpuMask . It is defined in settings_core.json , but no source file in the pinned PAL reads its value, so it has no effect in this build. Check whether a bug is a missing barrier When rendering is wrong only sometimes, or only on some GPUs: a classic sign of a missing pipeline barrier. DbgBarrierPostCmdEnable,0x8FFFFFFF XGL inserts a full barrier after every command in the mask ( DbgBarrierPostCmd runs after draws, dispatches, copies and so on). If the bug disappears, a barrier is missing. Narrow it down with the individual bits, e.g. DbgBarrierDrawIndexed = 2 or DbgBarrierDispatch = 8. Setting: DbgBarrierPostCmdEnable . Read at: The setting's own description says it requires a driver built with VKI_ENABLE_DEBUG_BARRIERS=1 . Rule out a residency or eviction bug When a crash or corruption might be caused by memory being evicted or missing from a submission's BO list. AlwaysResident,1 PAL creates every BO with AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_NO_EVICT and stops building BO lists on submit (the alwaysResident checks in AllocateOrPinMemory and OsSubmit ). If the problem goes away, look at memory residency ( Topic 2 ). Setting: AlwaysResident . Read at: , Zero new VRAM allocations (already the default here) When results depend on stale memory contents. InitializeVramToZero,1 XGL passes initializeToZero to PAL, which adds AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_VRAM_CLEARED to VRAM allocations. The upstream kernel checked out here already sets that flag on every GEM create (its comment says \"always clear VRAM\"), so with this kernel new VRAM is zeroed either way and the setting changes nothing. Setting: InitializeVramToZero (default true on Linux). Read at: , ,"}, {"t": "Shaders and pipelines", "p": "Use cases and settings", "u": "usecases.html#shaders", "x": "Dump every pipeline, its SPIR-V and the compiled code When a shader looks miscompiled, or you want the ISA of a hot pipeline. EnablePipelineDump,1 PipelineDumpDir,spvPipeline Every pipeline creation writes a .pipe file (the full Vkgc build info) and the SPIR-V of its shaders, named by pipeline hash. The README says the directory is relative to $AMD_DEBUG_DIR , $TMPDIR or /var/tmp . A .pipe file can be recompiled offline with LLPC's standalone amdllpc tool. Settings: EnablePipelineDump , PipelineDumpDir . Read at: . See Replace one shader without rebuilding the application When you want to test a fix to a shader you can't easily rebuild. ShaderReplaceMode,1 ShaderReplaceDir,ShaderReplace With mode 1 ( ShaderReplaceShaderHash ), for a shader whose hash is AAA, a file shader_0xAAA_replace.spv in the replacement directory is used instead of the original SPIR-V. Other modes replace by pipeline hash (2, 3), by ISA (4), or both (5). Get the hashes from a pipeline dump first. Settings: ShaderReplaceMode , ShaderReplaceDir . Read at: Measure cold compile times or rule out a stale cache When first-run stutter matters, or a bug might come from a cached binary. ShaderCacheMode,0 Mode 0 ( ShaderCacheDisable ) stops XGL from giving LLPC its internal cache adapter, so caches 3 and 4 on the shaders page stay empty. Mode 2 ( ShaderCacheEnableOnDisk ) persists the cache to disk instead. Setting: ShaderCacheMode . Read at: Compare NGG against the legacy geometry path (GFX10 only) When a geometry-heavy workload behaves differently than expected on GFX10. EnableNgg,0 EnableNgg is a bitmask of pipeline types: VS/FS = 1, GS = 2, tessellation = 4, tessellation + GS = 8 (default 1 | 4). XGL sets nggState.enableNgg per pipeline from it. On GFX11 and later LGC enables NGG regardless ( the shaders page ). Setting: EnableNgg . Read at:"}, {"t": "Performance", "p": "Use cases and settings", "u": "usecases.html#perf", "x": "Find out whether you are CPU-bound When you need to know how fast the application would run with an infinitely fast GPU. IFH,1 Infinitely Fast Hardware. With IfhModePal (1), PAL replaces every submitted command buffer with a dummy one, so the GPU does almost no work. If the frame rate hardly changes, the bottleneck is on the CPU. Mode 2 ( IfhModeKmd ) instead creates kernel contexts with AMDGPU_CTX_FLAGS_IFH ; that flag comes from PAL's own extension header ( amdgpu_drm_exts.h ) and is not in the upstream kernel uAPI checked out here, so use mode 1 on upstream kernels. Setting: IFH . Read at: , Time every draw on the GPU When you need per-draw or per-command-buffer GPU timings without an external profiler. GpuProfilerMode,1 GpuProfilerConfig.Granularity,0 GpuProfilerConfig.StartFrame,100 GpuProfilerConfig.FrameCount,1 PAL inserts its GpuProfiler layer between XGL and the core, which records timestamps around each command. Granularity 0 is per draw, 1 per command buffer, 2 per frame. The README describes the CSV output and a timingReport.py script that summarizes it. Modes 2 and 3 also capture thread traces. Settings: GpuProfilerMode , GpuProfilerConfig.Granularity , GpuProfilerConfig.StartFrame , GpuProfilerConfig.FrameCount . Read at: . See Watch frame times and memory per heap live When you want an on-screen view of FPS, CPU and GPU frame time, and how much memory each heap holds. DebugOverlayEnabled,1 PAL wraps its platform in the debug-overlay layer, which draws on presented images. The memory ledger maps directly onto the heaps on the memory page . Setting: DebugOverlayEnabled . Read at: . See Make CPU writes to GART memory write-combined When an application maps a HOST_CACHED memory type only to stream data to the GPU. OverrideHeapGartCacheableToUswc,1 XGL silently moves allocations from GpuHeapGartCacheable to GpuHeapGartUswc . CPU writes become write-combined and fast, but CPU reads from those mappings become very slow, so only use it for write-only uploads. Setting: OverrideHeapGartCacheableToUswc . Read at: Stop repeated map/unmap from costing mmap calls When an application maps and unmaps the same memory every frame. SkipUnMapMemory,1 XGL keeps the first CPU mapping and returns it on later vkMapMemory calls, avoiding a DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_MMAP plus mmap each time. Setting: SkipUnMapMemory . Read at:"}, {"t": "Use cases for learning the codebase", "p": "Use cases and settings", "u": "usecases.html#learning", "x": "Answer \"what does this Vulkan call do in the driver?\" Find it in the entry-point table ; its first call takes you to the XGL method, and the topic pages take it further down. Answer \"which ioctl does this end in?\" The PAL \u2192 libdrm tab maps each libdrm function PAL calls to the ioctl its body issues and to the kernel handler. Read an unfamiliar PM4 dump. Look up the opcode byte in the PM4 tab , then use the packet widget to decode a header. Understand a compile-time difference between GPUs. The compiler passes tab shows which LGC passes are conditional on the GFX level."}, {"t": "Code reference \u2013 AMDVLK Driver Atlas", "p": "Code reference", "u": "reference.html", "x": "Code reference Lookup tables extracted from the sources by tools/extract.py , one row per item, each linked to its definition at the pinned commit. Use the tabs, filter as you type, and link to any row: the address bar keeps the tab and filter. The reference needs JavaScript and assets/data/reference.js ."}, {"t": "How these tables are made, and their limits", "p": "Code reference", "u": "reference.html#how-made", "x": "Entry points: names come from XGL's DispatchTable::Init . \"First call in its body\" is the first method call found in the implementation, resolved to its class where the handle conversion makes that clear. It is a pointer for reading, not a full call graph. PAL interfaces: public methods of the I* classes in pal/inc/core , with the first sentence of each doc comment. Settings: every setting, and every field of struct settings, in XGL's and PAL's settings files. Defaults are shown as written there; some differ per OS. libdrm and ioctls: ioctl names are the ones literally present in each function body. Calls through helpers show up under \"Calls\" rather than \"Ioctls\". PM4 opcodes: \"refs\" counts lines in each hardware layer that mention the opcode, including table entries. Extensions: the condition shown is the nearest enclosing if ; deeper conditions and runtime checks elsewhere are not captured."}, {"t": "The loader finds the driver through a JSON manifest", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-1", "x": "The Khronos loader scans ICD manifest directories and reads  library_path . The  @AMDVLK_INSTALL_PATH@ / @ISABITS@  placeholders are filled in at build time (typically  amdvlk64.so ). The same file also declares an implicit AMD switchable-graphics layer. icd/Loader/LunarG/Lnx/amd-icd.json library_path"}, {"t": "Interface-version handshake", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-2", "x": "The first call the loader makes into the ICD. XGL accepts loader/ICD interface versions 3 to 6 and returns the highest one both sides support. Version 3+ means handles are dispatchable objects whose first word the loader owns. icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp MinDriverSupportedInterfaceVersion MaxDriverSupportedInterfaceVersion"}, {"t": "vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-3", "x": "The ICD-exported symbol the loader uses to fetch every other entry point. It just forwards to XGL's internal  vk::entry::vkGetInstanceProcAddr . icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp vk_icdGetInstanceProcAddr vk::entry::vkGetInstanceProcAddr"}, {"t": "Global vs. instance dispatch table", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-4", "x": "With no instance (e.g. looking up  vkCreateInstance ), XGL answers from the static  g_GlobalDispatchTable . Once an instance exists, it uses that instance's own table, which takes the enabled API version and extensions into account. icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp g_GlobalDispatchTable GetDispatchTable"}, {"t": "Name \u2192 function pointer lookup", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-5", "x": "A linear scan of  g_EntryPointMetadataTable  by name. Each entry is typed GLOBAL, INSTANCE, PHYSDEVICE or DEVICE, and the table type decides which kinds may be returned. That is how  vkGetDeviceProcAddr  refuses instance-level functions unless the  lenientInstanceFuncQuery  setting is on. icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp g_EntryPointMetadataTable EntryPoint::Type::GLOBAL EntryPoint::Type::PHYSDEVICE"}, {"t": "Filling the dispatch table", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-6", "x": " INIT_DISPATCH_ENTRY(vkAllocateMemory)  stores XGL's  vk::entry::vkAllocateMemory  in the table slot for that name. These  entry::  functions are XGL's real implementations, found in files such as  vk_device.cpp ,  vk_queue.cpp  and  entry.cpp . icd/api/vk_dispatch.cpp INIT_DISPATCH_ENTRY(vkAllocateMemory"}, {"t": "Dispatchable<C>: the loader owns the first word", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-7", "x": "A  VkDevice ,  VkQueue  or  VkCommandBuffer  handle points at a  Dispatchable<C> . Its first member,  VK_LOADER_DATA , starts as  ICD_LOADER_MAGIC  and is later overwritten by the loader with its dispatch pointer. The real driver object C sits right after it in raw storage, so C can use virtual functions and inheritance freely. icd/api/include/vk_dispatch.h VK_LOADER_DATA ICD_LOADER_MAGIC"}, {"t": "A typical entry point: handle \u2192 object \u2192 method", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-8", "x": "Almost every XGL entry point has this shape: convert the handle with  ApiDevice::ObjectFromHandle  (pointer arithmetic past the loader word), choose allocation callbacks, and call a method on the C++ object. icd/api/vk_device.cpp ApiDevice::ObjectFromHandle AllocMemory"}, {"t": "Instance creates the PAL platform", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-9", "x": " vkCreateInstance  ends up creating one  Pal::IPlatform . PAL uses the same \"ask the size, then construct in caller memory\" pattern everywhere:  Pal::GetPlatformSize()  first, then  Pal::CreatePlatform  into that memory. icd/api/vk_instance.cpp Pal::CreatePlatform m_pPalPlatform"}, {"t": "One Pal::IDevice per GPU", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-10", "x": " vkEnumeratePhysicalDevices  asks the PAL platform for its devices. On Linux each  Pal::IDevice  is an  Amdgpu::Device  that has opened a DRM render node and called  amdgpu_device_initialize . icd/api/vk_physical_device_manager.cpp EnumerateDevices"}, {"t": "XGL objects hold PAL objects", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-11", "x": "A logical  Device  keeps per-GPU state in  m_perGpu[] ; a device group has several GPUs. The same idea appears all over XGL:  Memory::PalMemory(idx) ,  CmdBuffer::PalCmdBuffer(idx) ,  Fence::PalFence(idx) . icd/api/include/vk_device.h m_perGpu[idx].pPalDevice"}, {"t": "Vulkan object and PAL object in one allocation", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-12", "x": "XGL asks PAL how big its object is ( GetGpuMemorySize ), makes one allocation through the app's  VkAllocationCallbacks  that holds both  sizeof(Memory)  and N PAL objects, and lets PAL construct its object in place. PAL never calls malloc for API objects. icd/api/vk_memory.cpp GetGpuMemorySize AllocApiObject"}, {"t": "PAL picks a hardware layer", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-13", "x": "Inside PAL, a  GfxDevice  is created for the detected GFX IP. Note the naming trap:  Gfx9::CreateDevice  handles  GFX10.1 through GFX11.5 . The directory is called gfx9 for historical reasons. Only GFX12 has its own HWL. src/core/device.cpp Gfx9::CreateDevice Gfx12::CreateDevice GfxIp11_5"}, {"t": "XGL derives the compiler's GFX IP from PAL", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-14", "x": " PipelineCompiler::Initialize  converts PAL's  GfxIpLevel  into a  Vkgc::GfxIpVersion  {major, minor, stepping}. LGC later turns that into an LLVM CPU name such as  gfx1100 . icd/api/pipeline_compiler.cpp m_gfxIp.major = 11 m_gfxIp.major = 12"}, {"t": "Where LLPC is created", "p": "Tracer: Loader \u2192 XGL \u2192 PAL (startup and object wrapping)", "u": "architecture.html#step-15", "x": "Each physical device owns one  Llpc::ICompiler , created with the GFX IP and a list of LLVM-style options. From here on, every shader module and pipeline build goes through  m_pLlpc . icd/api/compiler_solution_llpc.cpp Llpc::ICompiler::Create m_pLlpc"}, {"t": "vkAllocateMemory", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-1", "x": "The entry point unwraps the  VkDevice  and forwards to  Device::AllocMemory , which forwards to the static  Memory::Create . icd/api/vk_device.cpp AllocMemory"}, {"t": "Memory type index \u2192 PAL heap list", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-2", "x": "The app's  memoryTypeIndex  selects the preferred PAL heap through a table built at device init. XGL may then append fallback heaps, for example  GpuHeapGartUswc  after Local/Invisible when overallocation is allowed. (On Linux, PAL's amdgpu backend uses only  heaps[0]  to pick the kernel domain; see the domain-table step.) icd/api/vk_memory.cpp GetPalHeapFromVkTypeIndex Pal::GpuHeapGartUswc"}, {"t": "How Vulkan heaps are derived from PAL heaps", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-3", "x": "At device init XGL walks PAL's four heaps in a fixed priority order and creates one  VkMemoryHeap  per heap with nonzero size.  GartCacheable is never a heap of its own : it shares GartUswc's heap, because both are the same system memory with different CPU caching. icd/api/vk_physical_device.cpp Pal::GpuHeapInvisible Pal::GpuHeapGartUswc Pal::GpuHeapLocal Pal::GpuHeapGartCacheable"}, {"t": "How Vulkan memory-type flags are derived", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-4", "x": "Each PAL heap becomes one memory type, and its flags come straight from PAL's heap properties.  cpuVisible  \u2192 HOST_VISIBLE,  cpuGpuCoherent  \u2192 HOST_COHERENT, not  cpuUncached  \u2192 HOST_CACHED (GartCacheable). A device-local heap \u2192 DEVICE_LOCAL. With PAL's default heap flags ( Device::InitMemoryHeapProperties ) a discrete GPU gets: Invisible = DEVICE_LOCAL; Local = DEVICE_LOCAL|HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT (the BAR window, or all of VRAM with Resizable BAR); GartUswc = HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT; GartCacheable = HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT|HOST_CACHED. icd/api/vk_physical_device.cpp VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_CACHED_BIT VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT"}, {"t": "Create the PAL object and add it to the residency list", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-5", "x": "For each GPU in the allocation mask XGL calls  Pal::IDevice::CreateGpuMemory  into placement memory. It then immediately calls  Device::AddMemReference . Vulkan has no explicit residency API, so XGL makes  every   VkDeviceMemory  resident for the device's whole lifetime. icd/api/vk_memory.cpp CreateGpuMemory AddMemReference"}, {"t": "Residency: AddGpuMemoryReferences", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-6", "x": "A  Pal::GpuMemoryRef  goes to  IDevice::AddGpuMemoryReferences  with  pQueue == nullptr , meaning a  device-global  reference. On amdgpu this lands in every queue's global reference map, which becomes the BO list of every later submission. Topic 5 shows the other end. icd/api/vk_device.cpp AddGpuMemoryReferences Pal::GpuMemoryRef"}, {"t": "PAL core: construct + Init", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-7", "x": " ConstructGpuMemoryObject  is virtual: on Linux it placement-news an  Amdgpu::GpuMemory . The generic  GpuMemory::Init  computes size, alignment and VA partition, then calls the OS-specific  AllocateOrPinMemory . src/core/device.cpp ConstructGpuMemoryObject Init(createInfo, internalInfo)"}, {"t": "Virtual vs. real allocations", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-8", "x": "PAL distinguishes  virtual  allocations ( flags.virtualAlloc ), which are only a VA range with no physical backing, used for sparse resources, from ordinary ones. Every allocation gets a VA. Only non-virtual ones also get a BO. src/core/gpuMemory.cpp m_desc.flags.isVirtual virtualAlloc"}, {"t": "Generic Init hands off to the OS layer", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-9", "x": "By now  baseVirtAddr  is either 0 (\"pick one for me\"), a start-VA hint, or a capture/replay address. Everything OS-specific happens in  AllocateOrPinMemory . src/core/gpuMemory.cpp AllocateOrPinMemory"}, {"t": "Step A: reserve a GPU virtual address", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-10", "x": "Unless the VA was pre-reserved (multi-GPU global VA) or this is an SVM allocation, PAL first reserves VA space with  Device::AssignVirtualAddress . So far no physical memory exists. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuGpuMemory.cpp AssignVirtualAddress IsVirtual() == false"}, {"t": "AssignVirtualAddress \u2192 amdgpu_va_range_alloc", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-11", "x": "For the default partition PAL calls libdrm's  amdgpu_va_range_alloc  (through  m_drmProcs ) and keeps the returned  amdgpu_va_handle . Descriptor-table and capture/replay partitions use PAL's own VAM manager instead. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp pfnAmdgpuVaRangeAlloc amdgpu_gpu_va_range_general"}, {"t": "libdrm: VA allocation is pure user-space bookkeeping", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-12", "x": " Key point:   amdgpu_va_range_alloc  just carves a range out of libdrm's in-process allocator ( dev->va_mgr ). There is  no ioctl . The kernel learns about this VA only when a BO is mapped there with  AMDGPU_GEM_VA . amdgpu/amdgpu_vamgr.c amdgpu_va_range_alloc2 va_mgr"}, {"t": "Step B: PAL heap \u2192 kernel domain + flags", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-13", "x": "Where the four PAL heaps meet the kernel's two domains. Local and Invisible are both  AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM ; the difference is a  hint ,  CPU_ACCESS_REQUIRED  vs.  NO_CPU_ACCESS . Both GART heaps are  AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT ; the difference is  CPU_GTT_USWC  (write-combined) vs. 0 (cached). With Resizable BAR (invisible heap size 0), Invisible is treated like Local. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuGpuMemory.cpp AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_NO_CPU_ACCESS AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC"}, {"t": "Allocate physical backing, then map it at the VA", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-14", "x": "Physical backing:  AllocBuffer  creates the BO. Then  MapVirtualAddress  binds it at the VA from step A. These three operations (VA reservation, BO creation, mapping) are independent on amdgpu, which is what makes sparse binding possible. Earlier, PAL may add  AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_VM_ALWAYS_VALID  for BOs private to this process, so they never need to appear in a BO list. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuGpuMemory.cpp AllocBuffer MapVirtualAddress"}, {"t": "Device::AllocBuffer", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-15", "x": "A one-liner around libdrm's  amdgpu_bo_alloc . Failure is reported as  ErrorOutOfGpuMemory , which XGL turns into  VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY . src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp pfnAmdgpuBoAlloc"}, {"t": "libdrm: amdgpu_bo_alloc \u2192 DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-16", "x": "Fills  drm_amdgpu_gem_create  (size, alignment, domains, flags) and issues the ioctl. The kernel returns a GEM handle, which libdrm wraps in an  amdgpu_bo_handle . amdgpu/amdgpu_bo.c DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE drmCommandWriteRead"}, {"t": "Kernel: amdgpu_gem_create_ioctl", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-17", "x": "The kernel validates flags and domains and always clears VRAM. A  VM_ALWAYS_VALID  BO shares the reservation object of the process's VM root BO, which is why it needs no BO list. Then  amdgpu_gem_object_create  \u2192  amdgpu_bo_create_user  creates the TTM BO (placement details are not traced here). drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_gem.c amdgpu_gem_object_create vm->root.bo"}, {"t": "Device::MapVirtualAddress", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-18", "x": "Maps the BO at the chosen VA with read/write/execute permission plus an MTYPE (cache policy) derived from the PAL memory's  MType . The MALL no-alloc flag is added when the policy says  Never . src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp AMDGPU_VA_OP_MAP pfnAmdgpuBoVaOpRaw"}, {"t": "libdrm: amdgpu_bo_va_op_raw \u2192 DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_VA", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-19", "x": "This is the first time the kernel hears about the VA:  va_address ,  offset_in_bo  and  map_size  are sent with  AMDGPU_VA_OP_MAP . amdgpu/amdgpu_bo.c DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_VA va.va_address"}, {"t": "Kernel: amdgpu_gem_va_ioctl \u2192 amdgpu_vm_bo_map", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-20", "x": "The kernel records the mapping in the process VM ( amdgpu_vm_bo_map ). Shortly after this switch,  amdgpu_gem_va_update_vm  writes the page-table entries, unless user space asked for  AMDGPU_VM_DELAY_UPDATE . drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_gem.c amdgpu_vm_bo_map"}, {"t": "vkMapMemory \u2192 Memory::Map", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-21", "x": "Host-visible memory is mapped on demand. Multi-instance allocations cannot be mapped. With the  skipUnMapMemory  setting, XGL keeps the CPU pointer for the object's lifetime. icd/api/vk_memory.cpp PalMemory(m_primaryDeviceIndex)->Map"}, {"t": "PAL GpuMemory::Map: only CPU-visible heaps map", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-22", "x": "Virtual memory returns  ErrorUnavailable , and non-CPU-visible memory (the Invisible heap) returns  ErrorNotMappable . That is why only HOST_VISIBLE memory types can be mapped in Vulkan. Pinned host memory just returns the app's own pointer. src/core/gpuMemory.cpp IsCpuVisible OsMap ErrorNotMappable"}, {"t": "Amdgpu::Device::Map \u2192 amdgpu_bo_cpu_map", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-23", "x": " Amdgpu::GpuMemory::OsMap  calls this with the BO handle. For memory shared across devices in one process, it uses the  exporting  device's fd (the comment in  OsMap  explains the reverse-mapping problem). src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp pfnAmdgpuBoCpuMap"}, {"t": "libdrm: GEM_MMAP for an offset, then mmap(2)", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-24", "x": "Two steps.  DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_MMAP  returns a fake file offset that identifies the BO, then  drm_mmap  maps the DRM fd at that offset. Mappings are reference-counted per BO. CPU page faults are served by the kernel from VRAM (through the BAR) or from system pages. amdgpu/amdgpu_bo.c DRM_AMDGPU_GEM_MMAP drm_mmap"}, {"t": "Kernel: amdgpu_gem_mmap_ioctl", "p": "Tracer: vkAllocateMemory \u2192 GEM_CREATE + GEM_VA", "u": "memory.html#step-25", "x": "Just returns the BO's mmap offset ( amdgpu_mode_dumb_mmap ). The actual mapping is created when user space calls mmap on the DRM fd. The TTM fault path is  not traced  here. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_gem.c amdgpu_mode_dumb_mmap"}, {"t": "vkCreateShaderModule", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-1", "x": "Forwards through  Device::CreateShaderModule  to  ShaderModule::Create , which copies the SPIR-V and calls  ShaderModule::Init . icd/api/vk_device.cpp CreateShaderModule"}, {"t": "ShaderModule::Init", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-2", "x": "The SPIR-V words are wrapped in a  Vkgc::BinaryData  and handed to the per-device  PipelineCompiler . No ISA is generated here. In Vulkan the real work happens at pipeline creation, when all the state is known. icd/api/vk_shader.cpp BuildShaderModule Vkgc::BinaryData"}, {"t": "Shader-module cache", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-3", "x": " Cache #1 (shader modules) , keyed by a hash of the SPIR-V: an in-memory  m_shaderModuleHandleMap , then the internal  m_pBinaryCache . On a miss it calls LLPC and stores the result. icd/api/pipeline_compiler.cpp LoadShaderModuleFromCache StoreShaderModuleToCache m_compilerSolutionLlpc.BuildShaderModule"}, {"t": "Into LLPC: BuildShaderModule", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-4", "x": "XGL supplies an output allocator callback ( AllocateShaderOutput ) so LLPC's output lives in XGL-owned memory. icd/api/compiler_solution_llpc.cpp m_pLlpc->BuildShaderModule pfnOutputAlloc"}, {"t": "LLPC: hash, check cache, preprocess SPIR-V", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-5", "x": "LLPC hashes the SPIR-V with MetroHash and checks its own internal cache. On a miss (further down in the function) it identifies the binary type and collects usage info into a  ShaderModuleData . It still does not translate to LLVM IR. llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp CacheAccessor MetroHash64::Hash"}, {"t": "vkCreateGraphicsPipelines", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-6", "x": " Device::CreateGraphicsPipelines  loops over the create infos and calls  GraphicsPipelineCommon::Create  for each. icd/api/vk_device.cpp CreateGraphicsPipelines"}, {"t": "Library or full pipeline", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-7", "x": "Graphics pipeline libraries ( VK_EXT_graphics_pipeline_library ) take a separate path. This trace follows the monolithic  GraphicsPipeline::Create . icd/api/graphics_pipeline_common.cpp GraphicsPipeline::Create GraphicsPipelineLibrary::Create"}, {"t": "Pipeline binary cache lookup", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-8", "x": " Cache #2 (pipeline ELF) :  GetCachedPipelineBinary  checks the app's  VkPipelineCache  first, then XGL's internal  PipelineBinaryCache . If the ELF is found, LLPC is never called. icd/api/vk_graphics_pipeline.cpp GetCachedPipelineBinary shouldCompile"}, {"t": "Vulkan state \u2192 Vkgc::GraphicsPipelineBuildInfo", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-9", "x": "On a miss, XGL converts all relevant Vulkan state into LLPC's input: vertex input formats, color attachment formats, blend, resource mapping (descriptor layout \u2192 user-data nodes), shader stages and specialization constants. LGC's pipeline-state-dependent passes consume this state. icd/api/pipeline_compiler.cpp ConvertGraphicsPipelineInfo"}, {"t": "PipelineCompiler \u2192 compiler solution", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-10", "x": "XGL has a \"compiler solution\" abstraction. In this open-source tree the only one is  CompilerSolutionLlpc . icd/api/pipeline_compiler.cpp GetSolution(pCreateInfo->compilerType)->CreateGraphicsPipelineBinary"}, {"t": "Hand-off to LLPC: BuildGraphicsPipeline", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-11", "x": "Unless pre-compiled library ELFs are being linked, XGL asks LLPC to compile the whole pipeline in one go. icd/api/compiler_solution_llpc.cpp m_pLlpc->BuildGraphicsPipeline"}, {"t": "LLPC's own pipeline cache", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-12", "x": " Cache #3 (LLPC internal) : a whole-pipeline cache keyed by  cacheHash , which is the pipeline hash including state that affects code generation. On a miss LLPC builds a  GraphicsContext  and compiles. llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp cacheAccessor buildGraphicsPipelineInternal"}, {"t": "Create the LGC pipeline and give it the state", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-13", "x": " buildPipelineInternal  creates an  lgc::Pipeline  and copies the Vulkan pipeline state into it ( setPipelineState ). It also creates the  lgc::Builder  that the SPIR-V reader uses to emit LGC operations instead of raw hardware-specific IR. llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp createPipeline setPipelineState createBuilder"}, {"t": "Per stage: SPIR-V translation pass", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-14", "x": "For each shader stage, LLPC runs a small pass manager whose main pass is  LowerTranslator . It turns that stage's SPIR-V into an LLVM module. llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp LowerTranslator"}, {"t": "LowerTranslator \u2192 readSpirv", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-15", "x": "Before this, the function optionally runs a SPIR-V optimizer, builds the specialization-constant map and collects YCbCr converting samplers. Then the SPIRV reader does the translation. The excerpt is from  LowerTranslator::translateSpirvToLlvm . llpc/lowering/LowerTranslator.cpp readSpirv"}, {"t": "SPIRV reader: parse, then SPIRVToLLVM", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-16", "x": "Parses the binary into a  SPIRVModule  ( is >> *bm ), then  SPIRVToLLVM::translate  walks it and emits LLVM IR. The translator is given the  lgc::Builder , so image, built-in and similar operations come out as LGC builder calls rather than hardware-specific IR. LGC lowers them later, once it knows the pipeline state. llpc/translator/lib/SPIRV/SPIRVReader.cpp SPIRVToLLVM btl btl.translate"}, {"t": "SPIRVToLLVM::translate", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-17", "x": "Picks the requested entry point for this stage and starts translating. Only that entry point's call graph ends up in the stage's module. llpc/translator/lib/SPIRV/SPIRVReader.cpp getEntryPoint m_entryTarget"}, {"t": "Front-end lowering passes", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-18", "x": "API-level cleanups that don't depend on the GPU: structured-CFG merges, inline everything, lower SPIR-V access chains and terminators, then (further down) globals, memory ops and math. Afterwards the stage modules are linked into one pipeline module. llpc/lowering/Lowering.cpp LowerCfgMerges LowerAccessChain AlwaysInlinerPass"}, {"t": "LLPC \u2192 LGC: pipeline->generate()", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-19", "x": "One LLVM module now represents the whole pipeline.  generate  runs the LGC middle end and LLVM codegen and writes the ELF into  elfStream . llpc/context/llpcCompiler.cpp pipeline->generate"}, {"t": "PipelineState::generate: lower, then codegen", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-20", "x": "The two halves of the back end.  LgcLowering::addPasses  runs the hardware-aware middle-end passes on a new-PM pass manager.  addTargetPasses  then sets up the LLVM AMDGPU code generator on a legacy pass manager that writes the object file. lgc/state/Compiler.cpp LgcLowering::addPasses addTargetPasses"}, {"t": "Pipeline-state-dependent LGC passes", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-21", "x": "These passes are why AMDVLK compiles whole pipelines.  LowerVertexFetch  needs the vertex input formats,  LowerFragmentColorExport  needs the color attachment formats and blend state, and  LowerDesc  needs the descriptor layout.  CheckShaderCache  ( cache #4 , per-stage) can skip codegen for stages already compiled.  AddBufferOperationMetadata  runs only on GFX12+. lgc/lowering/LgcLowering.cpp LowerVertexFetch LowerFragmentColorExport LowerDesc CheckShaderCache AddBufferOperationMetadata"}, {"t": "GFX difference: NGG", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-22", "x": "On GFX11 and later, NGG (primitive shaders) is  mandatory ; the legacy VS/GS hardware path is gone. On GFX10 it is on unless disabled by a flag. Before this,  PreparePipelineAbi  has already merged API stages into hardware stages (e.g. VS+GS \u2192 one NGG primitive shader) and laid out the PAL ABI. lgc/lowering/LgcLowering.cpp usesNgg gfxIp.major >= 11"}, {"t": "GFX IP \u2192 LLVM CPU name", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-23", "x": "{11, 0, 0} becomes  gfx1100 , {12, 0, 1} becomes  gfx1201 . LGC accepts only names in its  GpuNameMap  table, which starts at  gfx1010 . lgc/state/LgcContext.cpp gpuNameStream << \"gfx\""}, {"t": "Create the AMDGPU TargetMachine", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-24", "x": "The triple is  amdgcn--amdpal : the AMDGPU backend with the PAL OS ABI. That ABI determines how the ELF's metadata note is written, and it is what PAL's loader expects. lgc/state/LgcContext.cpp amdgcn--amdpal lookupTarget"}, {"t": "Hand-off to the LLVM AMDGPU backend", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-25", "x": "From here, standard LLVM code generation (instruction selection, register allocation, scheduling) produces ISA for the chosen gfx target. Those internals are  not traced  on this site. lgc/state/LgcContext.cpp addPassesToEmitFile"}, {"t": "AsmPrinter writes PAL metadata", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-26", "x": "For each hardware-stage entry function, the backend records the entry symbol, VGPR/SGPR counts and (on older PAL ABI versions) the  RSRC1 / RSRC2  register values. This is how PAL later learns how to program the shader registers without understanding the ISA. llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/AMDGPUAsmPrinter.cpp setEntryPoint setNumUsedVgprs setNumUsedSgprs"}, {"t": "Metadata \u2192 ELF note", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-27", "x": "Modern PAL metadata is serialized as MsgPack into an ELF note. PAL reads it back with  PipelineAbiReader  and  MsgPackReader . llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/Utils/AMDGPUPALMetadata.cpp toMsgPackBlob"}, {"t": "XGL creates the PAL pipeline from the ELF", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-28", "x": "The ELF goes into  Pal::GraphicsPipelineCreateInfo::pPipelineBinary , along with dynamic-state-related info XGL collected, and PAL constructs an  IPipeline . icd/api/vk_graphics_pipeline.cpp pPipelineBinary CreateGraphicsPipeline"}, {"t": "PAL: route to the HWL", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-29", "x": "The core device forwards to the GFX device, which unpacks archive libraries if any and creates the HWL-specific pipeline object. src/core/device.cpp CreateGraphicsPipelineUnpackArchiveLibs"}, {"t": "GraphicsPipeline::Init from the binary", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-30", "x": "PAL keeps a copy of the ELF and calls  InitFromPipelineBinary , which reads the metadata (already unpacked by  PipelineAbiReader ) and calls the HWL's  HwlInit . src/core/hw/gfxip/graphicsPipeline.cpp InitFromPipelineBinary"}, {"t": "HWL: relocate + upload code, build registers", "p": "Tracer: VkShaderModule + vkCreateGraphicsPipelines \u2192 LLPC \u2192 ELF \u2192 PAL", "u": "shaders.html#step-31", "x": " CodeObjectUploader  copies the ELF's code and data sections into GPU memory ( pipelinePreferredHeap ) and applies relocations. The user-data layout and per-stage register values come from the metadata. The GFX10/11 HWL does the same in  Gfx9::GraphicsPipeline::HwlInit . src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx12/gfx12GraphicsPipeline.cpp CodeObjectUploader PerformRelocationsAndUploadToGpuMemory"}, {"t": "vkCreateCommandPool \u2192 ICmdAllocator", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-1", "x": "Each  VkCommandPool  owns a PAL  ICmdAllocator . It hands out fixed-size  chunks  of GPU memory for command data (PM4), embedded data (constants and descriptors written inline) and scratch.  allocSize  is the size of each GPU allocation and  suballocSize  the size of each chunk carved out of it.  autoMemoryReuse  lets chunks be recycled once the GPU is done with them. icd/api/vk_cmd_pool.cpp CommandDataAlloc EmbeddedDataAlloc suballocSize autoMemoryReuse"}, {"t": "vkBeginCommandBuffer", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-2", "x": "Unwraps the dispatchable  VkCommandBuffer  and calls  CmdBuffer::Begin , which resets XGL's tracked state and then begins the PAL command buffer. icd/api/entry.cpp ApiCmdBuffer::ObjectFromHandle"}, {"t": "Begin the PAL command buffer(s)", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-3", "x": "One PAL command buffer per GPU in the device mask.  ICmdBuffer::Begin  resets the PAL command streams. Their first chunk is requested lazily on the first command. icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp PalCmdBuffer(deviceIdx)->Begin"}, {"t": "vkCmdDraw", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-4", "x": "Note the argument re-ordering: Vulkan's (vertexCount, instanceCount, firstVertex, firstInstance) becomes XGL's (firstVertex, vertexCount, firstInstance, instanceCount). icd/api/entry.cpp ->Draw("}, {"t": "CmdBuffer::Draw: flush API state, then draw", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-5", "x": " ValidateGraphicsStates  pushes any Vulkan dynamic state that changed since the last draw (viewports, scissors, depth bias, etc.) into PAL calls. Then  PalCmdDraw  issues the draw. icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp ValidateGraphicsStates PalCmdDraw"}, {"t": "PalCmdDraw \u2192 ICmdBuffer::CmdDraw per GPU", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-6", "x": "For device groups, the draw is recorded into the PAL command buffer of every GPU in  m_curDeviceMask . icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp PalCmdBuffer(deviceIdx)->CmdDraw"}, {"t": "ICmdBuffer::CmdDraw is a function-pointer call", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-7", "x": "PAL's hot-path commands aren't virtual functions. They go through  m_funcTable , which the HWL fills with template instantiations specialized for the GFX level, whether PM4 optimization is on, whether view instancing is used, and so on. A draw ends up in a function with almost no runtime branches. inc/core/palCmdBuffer.h m_funcTable.pfnCmdDraw"}, {"t": "HWL CmdDraw: validate state", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-8", "x": "(GFX10\u201311 HWL, which lives in the gfx9 directory.)  ValidateDraw  writes every piece of dirty pipeline, user-data and render state as  SET_SH_REG / SET_CONTEXT_REG  packets. The exact register sequence is a large template and is  not traced  line by line here. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9UniversalCmdBuffer.cpp ValidateDraw<IsGfx11, false, Pm4OptEnabled>"}, {"t": "Reserve space, build the packet, commit", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-9", "x": " m_deCmdStream  is the draw-engine command stream.  ReserveCommands  returns a pointer into the current chunk with guaranteed headroom, packet builders write DWORDs through it, and  CommitCommands  advances the write pointer. Instance count and first vertex/instance were already written as registers during validation, so the packet only needs the vertex count. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9UniversalCmdBuffer.cpp BuildDrawIndexAuto CommitCommands"}, {"t": "CmdUtil::BuildDrawIndexAuto: the packet", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-10", "x": "Three DWORDs. DW0 is a type-3 header with opcode  IT_DRAW_INDEX_AUTO  (0x2D) and count = 3 \u2212 2 = 1. DW1 is  index_count . DW2 is a  VGT_DRAW_INITIATOR  value with SOURCE_SELECT = AUTO_INDEX. The annotated breakdown is below the tracer. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdUtil.cpp IT_DRAW_INDEX_AUTO DI_SRC_SEL_AUTO_INDEX draw_initiator"}, {"t": "Type3Header", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-11", "x": "Every type-3 packet goes through this helper.  count  is defined as the packet's total DWORD count minus 2, i.e. payload DWORDs \u2212 1. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdUtil.cpp header.count          = (count - 2); header.type           = 3;"}, {"t": "CmdStream::ReserveCommands", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-12", "x": "Reserves  ReserveLimit()  DWORDs. If the current chunk lacks the space, the stream chains to a new chunk. Chunks of one stream are linked so the CP can follow them. src/core/cmdStream.cpp ReserveLimit"}, {"t": "CmdStream::GetNextChunk", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-13", "x": "A stream first reuses its own  retained  chunks (kept across resets). Otherwise it asks the  CmdAllocator  for a fresh chunk. src/core/cmdStream.cpp m_retainedChunkList GetNewChunk"}, {"t": "CmdAllocator: free list \u2192 reuse list \u2192 new allocation", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-14", "x": "Chunk recycling. The free list holds chunks known to be idle. The reuse list holds chunks returned by reset command buffers; each is checked with  IsIdleOnGpu() , because the GPU may still be executing it. Only if both lists are exhausted does PAL make a new GPU allocation. src/core/cmdAllocator.cpp freeList reuseList IsIdleOnGpu busyList"}, {"t": "CmdAllocator::CreateAllocation", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-15", "x": "One  CmdStreamAllocation  (a GPU memory object of  allocSize ) is cut into  numChunks  chunks. The first is returned and the rest go on the free list. src/core/cmdAllocator.cpp allocCreateInfo.numChunks freeList.PushBack"}, {"t": "Compute: DISPATCH_DIRECT", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-16", "x": " vkCmdDispatch  \u2192  CmdBuffer::Dispatch  \u2192  ICmdBuffer::CmdDispatch  \u2192 HWL  CmdDispatch  \u2192 this builder. The packet carries the three group counts and a  COMPUTE_DISPATCH_INITIATOR ;  CS_W32_EN  selects wave32. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdUtil.cpp IT_DISPATCH_DIRECT dim_x dispatch_initiator"}, {"t": "vkCmdPipelineBarrier \u2192 release/acquire", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-17", "x": "On GFX10+ ( useReleaseAcquire ), XGL expresses Vulkan barriers in PAL's acquire/release model.  ExecuteAcquireRelease  translates stage and access masks plus image layouts and calls  ICmdBuffer::CmdReleaseThenAcquire . icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp m_flags.useReleaseAcquire ExecuteAcquireRelease"}, {"t": "GfxCmdBuffer \u2192 BarrierMgr", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-18", "x": "Barriers ignore predication. The HWL's  BarrierMgr  works out what the transition needs: waits on end-of-pipe events, cache write-back/invalidate, and metadata decompression blits. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfxCmdBuffer.cpp m_barrierMgr.ReleaseThenAcquire"}, {"t": "BarrierMgr::IssueReleaseThenAcquireSync", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-19", "x": "The source stage mask becomes  release events  (e.g. a RELEASE_MEM end-of-pipe event). The destination stage mask becomes an  acquire point  (how early the CP must wait), and the cache flags become GL2/GLx operations. These end up as RELEASE_MEM/ACQUIRE_MEM packets. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9AcquireReleaseBarrier.cpp GetAcquirePoint GetReleaseEvents ReserveCommands"}, {"t": "ACQUIRE_MEM packet", "p": "Tracer: vkBeginCommandBuffer \u2192 vkCmdDraw \u2192 PM4", "u": "cmdbuf.html#step-20", "x": " ACQUIRE_MEM  (0x58) makes the CP wait and/or perform cache actions before later work. Note the assert: on GFX11 the RB (color/depth) caches can no longer be flushed through ACQUIRE_MEM. That has to happen at release time. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdUtil.cpp IT_ACQUIRE_MEM coher_cntl isGfx11"}, {"t": "vkQueueSubmit", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-1", "x": "Both  vkQueueSubmit  and  vkQueueSubmit2  go to one template,  Queue::Submit<SubmitInfoType> . icd/api/vk_queue.cpp Submit<VkSubmitInfo>"}, {"t": "Wait semaphores first", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-2", "x": "In  PalWaitSemaphores , each wait becomes  IQueue::WaitQueueSemaphore  with a point value: 0 for binary semaphores, the requested value for timeline semaphores. On amdgpu this doesn't block. It queues the wait so the next CS ioctl carries it (see below). icd/api/vk_queue.cpp WaitQueueSemaphore pointValue"}, {"t": "Build the PAL SubmitInfo", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-3", "x": "XGL collects each  VkCommandBuffer 's  Pal::ICmdBuffer  into  ppCmdBuffers . Note  gpuMemRefCount = 0 : Vulkan never passes per-submit memory references, because everything was made resident at allocation time ( Device::AddMemReference ). icd/api/vk_queue.cpp Pal::PerSubQueueSubmitInfo gpuMemRefCount"}, {"t": "PalQueueSubmit \u2192 IQueue::Submit", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-4", "x": "Unless  multiSubmitChaining  is enabled, XGL submits command buffers one at a time and attaches the fence only to the last one. After the submit, signal semaphores go through  PalSignalSemaphores  \u2192  IQueue::SignalQueueSemaphore . icd/api/vk_queue.cpp pPalQueue->Submit(singleSubmitInfo) ppFences"}, {"t": "PAL core Queue: fences, then OsSubmit", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-5", "x": " IQueue::Submit  is  Queue::SubmitInternal  ( queue.h ). After validation and command-buffer bookkeeping, it associates each fence with the submission context and calls the OS layer. If the queue is stalled waiting on a semaphore, it enqueues the submit instead. src/core/queue.cpp AssociateWithContext OsSubmit"}, {"t": "Amdgpu::Queue::OsSubmit: residency + engine split", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-6", "x": "Unless  alwaysResident  is set, PAL builds the per-submit resource (BO) list from the device-global and per-queue references. Further down, universal/compute queues take  SubmitPm4  and DMA (SDMA) queues take  SubmitNonGfxIp . src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp UpdateResourceList"}, {"t": "UpdateResourceList: global refs \u2192 BO list", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-7", "x": "If nothing changed since the last submit, the previous list is reused. Otherwise the list is rebuilt from  m_globalRefMap , which holds every  VkDeviceMemory  that XGL added. Memory created  VM_ALWAYS_VALID  is skipped: the kernel keeps it valid in the process VM regardless. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp m_globalRefMap m_globalRefDirty"}, {"t": "Each command stream becomes an IB", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-8", "x": "PAL submits only the  first chunk  of each command stream as an IB, sized by  CmdDwordsToExecute() . The remaining chunks are reached by chaining packets that PAL wrote at the end of each chunk. SubmitPm4 also adds internal preamble/postamble streams around the app's command buffers. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp GetFirstChunk GpuVirtAddr AddIb"}, {"t": "AddIb: fill drm_amdgpu_cs_chunk_ib", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-9", "x": "An IB is just {GPU VA, size in bytes, IP type, ring, flags}. The IP type comes from the PAL engine type, which is how a submission reaches a  ring : GFX, COMPUTE or DMA. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp va_start ib_bytes ip_type GetIpType"}, {"t": "Engine \u2192 ring type", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-10", "x": "Universal (graphics) queues \u2192  AMDGPU_HW_IP_GFX , compute queues \u2192  AMDGPU_HW_IP_COMPUTE  (MEC/ACE rings), DMA queues \u2192  AMDGPU_HW_IP_DMA  (SDMA). The kernel's scheduler picks a hardware ring instance within the IP. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp AMDGPU_HW_IP_GFX AMDGPU_HW_IP_COMPUTE AMDGPU_HW_IP_DMA"}, {"t": "SubmitIbsRaw: IB chunks first", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-11", "x": "The CS ioctl takes an array of typed  chunks . IB chunks come first. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_IB"}, {"t": "Timeline-syncobj wait chunk", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-12", "x": "The semaphore waits queued earlier ( m_waitSemList ) become  AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT  entries of {syncobj handle, point}. A binary semaphore is a syncobj waited at point 0. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT m_waitSemList"}, {"t": "Signal chunk: always includes the queue's own syncobj", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-13", "x": "Every submission signals the queue's  m_lastSignaledSyncObject  (an extra entry after any PAL-internal signal semaphores). Fences and Vulkan signal semaphores are later fed from this syncobj. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp m_lastSignaledSyncObject AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_SIGNAL"}, {"t": "BO list: inline chunk (or legacy handle)", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-14", "x": "On current kernels the BO list goes inline as  AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES  (a  drm_amdgpu_bo_list_in  pointing to entries). On the legacy path, PAL first creates a list object with  DRM_AMDGPU_BO_LIST  and passes its handle. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES bo_info_ptr"}, {"t": "Device::SubmitRaw2", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-15", "x": "The returned sequence number ( pFence , stored as the context's last timestamp) identifies this submission. Legacy timestamp fences use it. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp pfnAmdgpuCsSubmitRaw2"}, {"t": "libdrm: amdgpu_cs_submit_raw2 \u2192 DRM_AMDGPU_CS", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-16", "x": "Packs the chunk pointers into  union drm_amdgpu_cs  together with the context ID and issues  DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS . amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c DRM_AMDGPU_CS cs.in.chunks cs.out.handle"}, {"t": "Kernel: amdgpu_cs_ioctl", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-17", "x": "The kernel side in one function.  pass1  copies and parses chunks;  pass2  handles IBs and syncobj dependencies;  parser_bos  validates and reserves every BO in the list (moving them into place if evicted);  vm_handling  makes sure page tables are current;  sync_rings  collects dependencies;  submit  queues the job. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c amdgpu_cs_pass1 amdgpu_cs_pass2 amdgpu_cs_parser_bos amdgpu_cs_vm_handling amdgpu_cs_sync_rings"}, {"t": "pass1: chunk parsing", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-18", "x": "The chunk IDs PAL produced are matched here: IB chunks become job IBs and  AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES  builds the submission's BO list. Syncobj chunks are handled in pass2 (e.g.  amdgpu_cs_p2_syncobj_timeline_wait , next step). drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_IB AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_BO_HANDLES"}, {"t": "pass2: timeline wait \u2192 job dependency", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-19", "x": "Each {handle, point} is looked up and its fence added as a dependency of the job. The GPU scheduler won't run the job until the fence signals, so waits cost no CPU time. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c amdgpu_syncobj_lookup_and_add"}, {"t": "Queue the job on the GPU scheduler", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-20", "x": " cs->out.handle = seq  is the sequence number libdrm returns to PAL.  drm_sched_entity_push_job  hands the job to the DRM GPU scheduler. The ioctl returns now; execution is asynchronous. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_cs.c drm_sched_entity_push_job cs->out.handle = seq"}, {"t": "Scheduler runs the job: amdgpu_job_run", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-21", "x": "Once dependencies are satisfied, the scheduler calls  amdgpu_job_run , which calls  amdgpu_ib_schedule  on the ring. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_job.c amdgpu_ib_schedule"}, {"t": "amdgpu_ib_schedule: emit IBs + fence", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-22", "x": "For each IB, the ring-specific  emit_ib  writes a packet into the kernel ring buffer. Then  amdgpu_fence_emit  writes an end-of-pipe fence, which is what eventually signals the syncobjs. drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/amdgpu_ib.c amdgpu_ring_emit_ib amdgpu_fence_emit"}, {"t": "gfx_v11: INDIRECT_BUFFER packet in the ring", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-23", "x": "The handoff to hardware. A PM4  INDIRECT_BUFFER  packet with PAL's IB address and the  VMID  in the control word (so the CP translates the IB's VAs through this process's page tables) is written to the ring. The CP then fetches and executes PAL's PM4, including the  DRAW_INDEX_AUTO  from Topic 4. GFX12's equivalent is  gfx_v12_0_ring_emit_ib_gfx . drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/gfx_v11_0.c PACKET3_INDIRECT_BUFFER vmid << 24"}, {"t": "VkFence \u2192 Pal::IFence", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-24", "x": "The same size-then-placement pattern. One PAL fence per GPU. icd/api/vk_fence.cpp GetFenceSize CreateFence"}, {"t": "amdgpu: SyncobjFence or legacy TimestampFence", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-25", "x": "On kernels with syncobj support (the normal case), a PAL fence wraps a DRM syncobj. The legacy  TimestampFence  instead waits on a (context, ring, sequence-number) triple. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp SyncobjFence TimestampFence"}, {"t": "Signal the fence from the submission's syncobj", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-26", "x": "After the CS ioctl, the fence's syncobj gets a copy of the fence just attached to the queue's  m_lastSignaledSyncObject  ( ConveySyncObjectState  \u2192  amdgpu_cs_syncobj_transfer ).  vkWaitForFences  then waits on the syncobj ( WaitForSyncobjFences ). src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp ConveySyncObjectState GetLastSignaledSyncObj"}, {"t": "VkSemaphore: binary or timeline", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-27", "x": "A  VkSemaphoreTypeCreateInfo  with  VK_SEMAPHORE_TYPE_TIMELINE  sets  Pal::QueueSemaphoreCreateInfo::flags.timeline  and the initial value. Otherwise it's binary. Both become a  Pal::IQueueSemaphore  via  CreateQueueSemaphore . icd/api/vk_semaphore.cpp palCreateInfo.flags.timeline VK_SEMAPHORE_TYPE_TIMELINE"}, {"t": "IQueueSemaphore = DRM syncobj", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-28", "x": "With  SemaphoreType::SyncObj , each semaphore, binary or timeline, is a DRM syncobj created by  CreateSyncObject  (\u2192  amdgpu_cs_create_syncobj2 ).  ProOnly  is a legacy amdgpu-pro semaphore type. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuDevice.cpp SemaphoreType::SyncObj CreateSyncObject"}, {"t": "Queue::WaitSemaphore just records the wait", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-29", "x": "For syncobj semaphores the wait is appended to  m_waitSemList  and becomes an  AMDGPU_CHUNK_ID_SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT  chunk of the next CS ioctl. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp m_waitSemList.PushBack"}, {"t": "Queue::SignalSemaphore transfers from the last submit", "p": "Tracer: vkQueueSubmit \u2192 DRM_IOCTL_AMDGPU_CS \u2192 ring", "u": "submit.html#step-30", "x": "Signalling (binary point 0 or a timeline point) copies the fence of the queue's last submission into the semaphore's syncobj at that point. If the queue has a pending wait, or has never submitted, PAL first does a dummy submission so there is a fence to transfer. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuQueue.cpp ConveySyncObjectState DummySubmit"}, {"t": "How big is each binding?", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-1", "x": "A descriptor set is a block of GPU memory holding hardware descriptors (SRDs). When the layout is created, every binding gets a size from PAL's SRD sizes: images use  imageView , texel buffers use  typedBufferView , uniform and storage buffers use  untypedBufferView .  Dynamic  buffers take no space in the set: their SRDs live in user-data registers instead (see the bind step). icd/api/vk_descriptor_set_layout.cpp descriptorSizes.imageView descriptorSizes.untypedBufferView VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_UNIFORM_BUFFER_DYNAMIC"}, {"t": "SRD sizes come from the hardware layer", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-2", "x": "On GFX10/11 a buffer SRD is  BufferSrd  (4 DWORDs) and an image SRD is  ImageSrd  (8 DWORDs). XGL reports the same values to applications through  VK_EXT_descriptor_buffer  properties, and they are the strides used inside a set. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9Device.cpp sizeof(BufferSrd) sizeof(ImageSrd)"}, {"t": "A pool is one CPU-mapped GPU allocation", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-3", "x": " vkCreateDescriptorPool  allocates the pool's memory once, from XGL's internal memory manager's descriptor-table pool ( InternalPoolDescriptorTable ), and keeps both its GPU address and a CPU pointer. Writing a descriptor is therefore a plain CPU store into GPU-visible memory. There is no command or ioctl per update. icd/api/vk_descriptor_pool.cpp InternalPoolDescriptorTable AllocGpuMem staticCpuAddr staticGpuAddr"}, {"t": "vkAllocateDescriptorSets carves a range out of the pool", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-4", "x": "A set's size is the layout's static section ( sta.dwSize ), adjusted for a variable-count last binding. Pools created  without   VK_DESCRIPTOR_POOL_CREATE_FREE_DESCRIPTOR_SET_BIT  use a bump allocator ( oneShot ), which is just an aligned offset increment. Pools that allow freeing go through a slower general allocator. icd/api/vk_descriptor_pool.cpp oneShot sta.dwSize VK_DESCRIPTOR_POOL_CREATE_FREE_DESCRIPTOR_SET_BIT"}, {"t": "vkUpdateDescriptorSets is specialised per device", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-5", "x": "The entry point jumps through the device's own entry-point table. At device creation XGL picks a template instantiation of the update function for this GPU's descriptor sizes and GPU count ( GetUpdateDescriptorSetsFunc ), so the per-descriptor work has no runtime size checks. icd/api/vk_descriptor_set.cpp GetEntryPoints().vkUpdateDescriptorSets"}, {"t": "Find the destination address inside the set", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-6", "x": "For each  VkWriteDescriptorSet , the destination is the set's CPU address plus the binding's offset in the static section plus the array element. The switch that follows picks a writer per descriptor type. icd/api/vk_descriptor_set.cpp StaticCpuAddress GetDstStaOffset"}, {"t": "Image descriptors: copy a pre-built SRD", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-7", "x": "Writing a sampled or storage image is a  memcpy  of an SRD that was built when the  VkImageView  was created. A null view writes zeros, which makes the hardware return zero for reads (null descriptors). icd/api/vk_descriptor_set.cpp memcpy(pDestAddr, pImageDesc, imageDescSize); memset(pDestAddr, 0, imageDescSize);"}, {"t": "\u2026built once, at vkCreateImageView", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-8", "x": " ImageView  asks PAL to encode the view (format, swizzle, mip and layer range, address and tiling of the image) into hardware form. Storage-image usage builds a second, read-write SRD next to it. icd/api/vk_image_view.cpp CreateImageViewSrds"}, {"t": "Buffer descriptors: built at update time", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-9", "x": "Uniform and storage buffers have no view object, so the SRD is encoded during the update: GPU address of the buffer plus offset, range (rounded up to a DWORD;  VK_WHOLE_SIZE  means to the end of the buffer), straight into the set's memory. icd/api/vk_descriptor_set.cpp CreateUntypedBufferViewSrds VK_WHOLE_SIZE"}, {"t": "PAL encodes a 4-DWORD buffer SRD", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-10", "x": "The whole descriptor is 128 bits. DW0 and DW1 hold the 48-bit address (and the stride in DW1), DW2 holds  NUM_RECORDS  (bytes for raw buffers), and DW3 holds the component swizzle, format, out-of-bounds mode and resource type. Try it in the SRD builder below. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9Device.cpp SqBufRsrcTWord1StrideShift CalcNumRecords SqBufRsrcTWord3DstSelXShift"}, {"t": "vkCmdBindDescriptorSets: dynamic SRDs go straight to user data", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-11", "x": "Binding records each set's GPU address in a per-command-buffer shadow ( setBindingData ). Dynamic uniform and storage buffers are patched with the dynamic offset right here and stored in the same shadow, so no memory write is needed per draw. icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp setBindingData PatchedDynamicDataFromHandle"}, {"t": "Set addresses become PAL user data", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-12", "x": "The pipeline layout decides which user-data entries each set occupies ( setBindingRegBase ). XGL writes the changed range with  CmdSetUserData , but only if the currently bound pipeline uses the same layout base. Otherwise the next  vkCmdBindPipeline  rewrites them. icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp setBindingRegBase CmdSetUserData"}, {"t": "PAL only records user data\u2026", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-13", "x": " CmdSetUserData  writes no PM4. It updates PAL's tracked copy of the user-data entries, filtering out values that didn't change, and marks them dirty. src/core/hw/gfxip/universalCmdBuffer.cpp FilterSetUserData gfxUserDataEntries"}, {"t": "\u2026and writes dirty entries into shader SGPRs at draw time", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-14", "x": "During  ValidateDraw , each hardware stage's user-data entries are copied into its user SGPR registers with  SET_SH_REG . The pipeline's metadata says which entry maps to which SGPR ( entryMap ), so the shader starts with the descriptor-table pointers already in scalar registers. src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9CmdStream.cpp BuildSetSeqShRegs entryMap.mappedEntry firstUserSgpr"}, {"t": "Shader side: LGC lowers a descriptor access", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-15", "x": "In the shader, a buffer access starts as  lgc.load.buffer.desc(set, binding, index) .  LowerDesc  replaces it with real code that loads the SRD and turns it into a buffer fat pointer. lgc/lowering/LowerDesc.cpp CreateBufferDesc op.getDescSet() op.getBinding()"}, {"t": "Which user-data entry holds set N?", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-16", "x": "The builder looks up (set, binding) in the  resource mapping  that XGL built from the pipeline layout ( BuildPipelineResourceMapping ). That gives the user-data entry holding the table pointer and the binding's offset, so the generated ISA becomes \"load 4 DWORDs from (SGPR pointer + offset + index \u00d7 stride)\". This is why pipelines depend on the pipeline layout. lgc/builder/DescBuilder.cpp findResourceNode ResourceNodeType::DescriptorBuffer"}, {"t": "Push constants are user data too", "p": "Tracer: Descriptor sets: layout \u2192 pool \u2192 update \u2192 bind \u2192 shader load", "u": "descriptors.html#step-17", "x": " vkCmdPushConstants  takes the same route.  WritePushConstants  copies the values into a per-bind-point shadow ( pushConstData ) and writes them with  CmdSetUserData  at the layout's  pushConstRegBase . A shader therefore reads push constants from user SGPRs without loading any descriptor. icd/api/vk_cmdbuffer.cpp PushConstantsIssueWrites"}, {"t": "vkCreateSwapchainKHR creates a PAL swap chain", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-1", "x": "XGL fills a  Pal::SwapChainCreateInfo  and creates a PAL  ISwapChain  with the size-then-create pattern. If PAL reports the window is already owned by another swap chain, that becomes  VK_ERROR_NATIVE_WINDOW_IN_USE_KHR . On Linux the PAL swap chain talks to the window system through a DRI3 (X11) or Wayland window-system object. icd/api/vk_swapchain.cpp GetSwapChainSize CreateSwapChain"}, {"t": "vkAcquireNextImageKHR", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-2", "x": "The Vulkan semaphore and fence to signal are translated to their PAL objects and handed to  ISwapChain::AcquireNextImage , together with the timeout. icd/api/vk_swapchain.cpp m_pPalSwapChain->AcquireNextImage PalSemaphore"}, {"t": "amdgpu swap chain: fast path for native window systems", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-3", "x": "Two paths. Normally  OptimizedHandlingForNativeWindowSystem  returns false and PAL's generic acquire runs (next step). When the native window system can hand back the image index itself, the fast path is taken, and outside mailbox mode PAL only has to arrange for the app's semaphore and fence to be signalled when that image's previous present completes ( SignalOnAcquire ). src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuSwapChain.cpp SignalOnAcquire Pal::SwapChain::AcquireNextImage"}, {"t": "Pick the least recently used free image", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-4", "x": "FIFO and immediate modes block on a counting semaphore of available images; mailbox mode searches for unused ones. The image returned is the  least recently used , not the lowest free index, because applications often key per-frame state off the index (the comment explains why). src/core/swapChain.cpp m_availableImageSemaphore.Wait m_unusedImageQueue[0]"}, {"t": "vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 IQueue::PresentSwapChain", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-5", "x": " Queue::Present  first turns the present's wait semaphores into queue waits ( PalWaitSemaphores ), then fills a  Pal::PresentSwapChainInfo  (image, index, mode) for each swap chain and calls  PresentSwapChain  on the presenting queue. icd/api/vk_queue.cpp PresentSwapChain(presentInfo)"}, {"t": "PAL validates the present", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-6", "x": "The image must be presentable (and flippable for fullscreen). Even if validation fails PAL must still release the image index, or a later acquire would deadlock (the comment explains). It also advances the frame counter. src/core/queue.cpp IsPresentable pSwapChain->Present(presentInfo, this) IncFrameCount"}, {"t": "SwapChain::Present hands off to the present scheduler", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-7", "x": "Unless the swap chain is in mailbox mode, an image whose present is still in flight can be handed back to the application early ( ReuseImage ) when  canAcquireBeforeSignaling  is set. src/core/swapChain.cpp m_pScheduler->Present ReuseImage"}, {"t": "Inline or on the present thread", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-8", "x": "If the present can be processed immediately it runs inline; otherwise it is queued as a job for PAL's present worker thread, which starts on first use. Presents stay ordered: switching back to inline first drains the thread. src/core/presentScheduler.cpp CanInlinePresent ProcessPresent m_workerThread.Begin"}, {"t": "Linux: ask the window system, then honour FIFO", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-9", "x": "Linux supports immediate, mailbox and FIFO. The window system is given an  idle fence  for the image. In FIFO mode PAL then waits until the request has been handed to hardware ( WaitForLastImagePresented ) before completing. src/core/os/amdgpu/amdgpuPresentScheduler.cpp m_pWindowSystem->Present WaitForLastImagePresented PresentComplete"}, {"t": "X11: xcb_present_pixmap", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-10", "x": "On X11 each swap-chain image was shared with the X server as a DRI3 pixmap. Presenting is one Present extension request naming that pixmap, with a wait fence and an  idle fence  the server signals when it no longer reads the image.  targetMsc / divisor / remainder  express vsync timing. src/core/os/amdgpu/dri3/dri3WindowSystem.cpp pfnXcbPresentPixmapChecked idleSyncFence targetMsc"}, {"t": "Explicit sync: timeline syncobjs shared with the compositor", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-11", "x": "When the server supports it, PAL uses explicit synchronization. Each image has an acquire and a release timeline syncobj (the same DRM syncobjs as Topic 5). The GPU signals the acquire point when rendering is done, and the compositor signals the release point when it is finished with the image. src/core/os/amdgpu/dri3/dri3WindowSystem.cpp SignalExplicitSyncAcquire pfnXcbPresentPixmapSyncedChecked acquirePoint"}, {"t": "Wayland: attach the buffer to the surface", "p": "Tracer: vkAcquireNextImageKHR \u2192 vkQueuePresentKHR \u2192 X11/Wayland", "u": "present.html#step-12", "x": "On Wayland each image is a  wl_buffer . Presenting attaches it to the surface, adds damage rectangles when the compositor supports them, and commits ( WL_SURFACE_COMMIT ). With the Linux DRM syncobj protocol ( WP_LINUX_DRM_SYNCOBJ_SURFACE_V1_SET_ACQUIRE_POINT ) PAL first sets the acquire and release timeline points, mirroring X11's explicit sync. src/core/os/amdgpu/wayland/waylandWindowSystem.cpp WL_SURFACE_ATTACH"}, {"t": "BO (Buffer Object)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-bo", "x": "A kernel-managed allocation of GPU-accessible memory (a DRM GEM object backed by TTM). Created by  AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE ; user space refers to it by a handle."}, {"t": "IB (Indirect Buffer)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-ib", "x": "A chunk of PM4 commands in GPU memory. User space submits IBs by GPU VA and size; the kernel emits an  INDIRECT_BUFFER  packet on a ring that points at it."}, {"t": "GART (Graphics Address Remapping Table)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-gart", "x": "System (host) memory made GPU-accessible through GPU page tables. The kernel calls this domain GTT. PAL exposes it as two heaps:  GpuHeapGartUswc  (write-combined) and  GpuHeapGartCacheable  (CPU-cached)."}, {"t": "GTT (Graphics Translation Table)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-gtt", "x": "The amdgpu kernel's name for the system-memory domain ( AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_GTT ). PAL's GART heaps map to it."}, {"t": "VRAM (Video RAM)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-vram", "x": "Device-local memory ( AMDGPU_GEM_DOMAIN_VRAM ). PAL splits it into  GpuHeapLocal  (CPU-visible BAR window) and  GpuHeapInvisible ."}, {"t": "USWC (Uncached Speculative Write-Combining)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-uswc", "x": "A CPU caching mode: writes are combined and streamed, and reads are uncached and slow. Good for CPU\u2192GPU upload buffers. Requested with  AMDGPU_GEM_CREATE_CPU_GTT_USWC ."}, {"t": "VA (GPU Virtual Address)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-va", "x": "An address in the process's GPU virtual address space. Reserving a VA (libdrm  amdgpu_va_range_alloc ) is separate from backing it with a BO and mapping it ( AMDGPU_GEM_VA )."}, {"t": "PM4 (Packet Manager 4 command format)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-pm4", "x": "The command format the GPU command processor parses. Type-3 packets have a 32-bit header (opcode, payload count, shader type, predicate) followed by payload DWORDs."}, {"t": "CP (Command Processor)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-cp", "x": "The GPU front-end microcontrollers (PFP/ME for graphics, MEC for compute) that fetch and execute PM4."}, {"t": "LLPC (LLVM-Based Pipeline Compiler)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-llpc", "x": "AMD's Vulkan pipeline compiler library. Its front end translates SPIR-V to LLVM IR; it also contains LGC and drives the LLVM AMDGPU backend."}, {"t": "LGC (LLPC middle end (\"LLVM-based Graphics Compiler\"))", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-lgc", "x": "LLPC's middle end ( llpc/lgc ). It lowers  lgc::Builder  operations into AMDGPU-ready IR using the pipeline state (vertex formats, color formats, descriptor layout, GFX IP) and emits the PAL pipeline ABI."}, {"t": "XGL (Vulkan API layer)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-xgl", "x": "The repo that implements the Vulkan ICD for AMDVLK ( icd/api ). It translates Vulkan calls into PAL calls."}, {"t": "PAL (Platform Abstraction Library)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-pal", "x": "AMD's shared GPU abstraction layer, used by several AMD drivers. It hides hardware generation (HWL) and OS (amdgpu) details."}, {"t": "HWL (Hardware Layer)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-hwl", "x": "PAL's per-GPU-generation implementation ( hw/gfxip/gfx9 ,  hw/gfxip/gfx12 ) that builds PM4 and register state."}, {"t": "ICD (Installable Client Driver)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-icd", "x": "A Vulkan driver loaded by the Khronos loader through a JSON manifest. AMDVLK's ICD is XGL."}, {"t": "syncobj (DRM synchronization object)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-syncobj", "x": "A kernel container for a dma_fence, optionally a timeline of fences indexed by a 64-bit point. Used for Vulkan fences, binary semaphores and timeline semaphores."}, {"t": "SDMA (System DMA engine)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-sdma", "x": "A copy engine with its own ring ( AMDGPU_HW_IP_DMA ). It runs PAL's DMA queues (Vulkan transfer queues)."}, {"t": "NGG (Next-Generation Geometry)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-ngg", "x": "The primitive-shader geometry pipeline of GFX10+. LGC generates it and makes it mandatory on GFX11+."}, {"t": "ELF (Executable and Linkable Format)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-elf", "x": "The container LLPC emits: ISA in .text plus a PAL metadata note (MsgPack) describing register values, user-data layout and resource usage."}, {"t": "SRD (Shader Resource Descriptor)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-srd", "x": "A 4- or 8-DWORD hardware descriptor for a buffer, image or sampler, read by shaders from descriptor memory."}, {"t": "SPIR-V (Standard Portable IR (Vulkan shader binary))", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-spirv", "x": "The shader IR an application passes to  vkCreateShaderModule . LLPC parses it with its SPIRV reader."}, {"t": "ISA (Instruction Set Architecture)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-isa", "x": "The GPU machine code (e.g. RDNA3 for gfx11) produced by the LLVM AMDGPU backend."}, {"t": "GEM (Graphics Execution Manager)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-gem", "x": "The DRM core's buffer-object framework. amdgpu BOs are GEM objects."}, {"t": "TTM (Translation Table Manager)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-ttm", "x": "The DRM memory manager that places and evicts BOs between VRAM and system memory. Not traced in detail on this site."}, {"t": "MEC (Micro Engine Compute)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-mec", "x": "The CP block that runs compute queues (ACE). PAL compute queues map to  AMDGPU_HW_IP_COMPUTE ."}, {"t": "residency (memory residency)", "p": "Glossary", "u": "glossary.html#g-residency", "x": "Guaranteeing that every BO a submission might touch is present and mapped while the GPU runs. On amdgpu this is done with the per-submit BO list, or by creating BOs  VM_ALWAYS_VALID ."}];
